l control of freshwater diatoms – a review. \nDiatom Research, 22(2), 473–490. \nSun, X., Wu, N., Hörmann, G., Faber, C., Messyasz, B., Qu, Y., and Fohrer, N., 2022. Using \nintegrated models to analyze and predict the variance of diatom community composition \nin an agricultural area. Science of the Total Environment, 803, 149894. \nTaylor, J.C., Harding, W.R., and Archibald, C.G.M., 2005. A Methods Manual for the \nCollection, Preparation and Analysis of Diatom Samples. Version 1.0. WRC Report No. \nTT 281/07. Water Research Commission, Pretoria. \nJournal Pre-proof\nJournal Pre-proof\n \n33 \n \nTaylor, J.C., Harding, W.R., and Archibald, C.G.M., 2007. An Illustrated Guide to some \nCommon Diatom Species from South Africa. WRC Report No. TT 282/07. Water \nResearch Commission, Pretoria. \nTimberlake, J., 1994. Physical features of the Chirinda forest. Chirinda Forest – a visitor’s \nguide. Forestry Commission, Harare. \nTonkin, J.D., Merritt, D.M., Olden, J.D., Reynolds, L.V. and Lytle, D.A., 2018. Flow regime \nalteration degrades ecological networks in riparian ecosystems. Nature Ecology and \nEvolution, 2(1), 86–93. \nVidal, T., Santos, M., Santos, J.I., Luís, A.T., Pereira, M.J., Abrantes, N., Gonçalves, F.J., \nand Pereira, J.L., 2021. Testing the response of benthic diatom assemblages to common \nriverine contaminants. Science of the Total Environment, 755, 142534. \nVörösmarty, C.J., McIntyre, P.B., Gessner, M.O., Dudgeon, D., Prusevich, A., Green, P., \nGlidden, S., Bunn, S.E., Sullivan, C.A., Liermann, C.R., and Davies, P.M., 2010. Global \nthreats to human water security and river biodiversity. Nature, 467(7315), 555–561. \nWalsh, G., and Wepener, V., 2009. The influence of land–use on water quality and diatom \ncommunity structures in urban and agriculturally stressed rivers. Water SA, 35, 579–594. \n \nJournal Pre-proof\nJournal Pre-proof\n \n34 \n \n \nGraphical abstract \nJournal Pre-proof\nJournal Pre-proof\n \n35 \n \nHighlights \n Mercury and pH were high in the national park sites. \n High phosphorus concentrations were in timber plantation and communal area sites. \n Communal and timber plantation sites were largely grouped together. \n Strong correlations were observed between diatom data and environmental variables. \n Results provide an important scientific reference for land use optimization and \nguidance for policy formulation. \nJournal Pre-proof\nJournal Pre-proof",
