 {
  "title": "Potential toxic elements in costal sediment of the Persian Gulf: a systematic review-meta-analysis, non-dietary risk assessment and ecological risk assessment",
  "url": "https://doi.org/10.1007/s11356-023-26949-8",
  "source": "Environmental Science and Pollution Research",
  "journal": "Environmental Science and Pollution Research",
  "journal_tier": "top",
  "date": "2023-04-25",
  "category": "literature",
  "department": "其他",
  "region": "国际",
  "quality": "C",
  "summary": "Potential toxic elements in costal sediment of the Persian Gulf: a systematic review-meta-analysis, non-dietary risk assessment and ecological risk assessment",
  "tags": [],
  "content_fetched": true,
  "pdf": "kb/pdf/dc1354b2ea911938.pdf",
  "cid": "dc1354b2ea911938",
  "importance": 5.59,
  "added_at": "2026-10-06",
  "_ev": "ti:ba81ba59f19e7fee",
  "quality_src": "B",
  "water_rel": 0.3,
  "kg_terms": [],
  "content_type": "研究文献",
  "kclass": "research",
  "content_class": "academic",
  "gate_status": "keep",
  "gate_reason": "学术文献（top：Environmental Science and Pollution Research）",
  "content": "Page 1/25\nPotential toxic elements (PTEs) in costal sediment of\nthe Persian Gulf: A systematic review-meta-analysis,\nnon-dietary risk Assessment and ecological risk\nassessment\nXiaomei Hui \nChina-Japan Union Hospital of Jilin University\nAmir Mohammad Sheikh Asadi \nGermany Federal Financial Administration Education and Science Center Münster: Bildungs- und\nWissenschaftszentrum der Bundesnanzverwaltung\nYadolah Fakhri  (  Ya.fakhri@gmail.com )\nHormozgan University of Medical Sciences School of Health https://orcid.org/0000-0002-3579-7641\nFereshteh Mehri \nHamedan University of Medical Sciences Department of Biostatistics and Epidemiology\nIntissar Limam \nTunis El Manar University: Universite de Tunis El Manar\nVan Nam Thai \nVietnam National University Hanoi\nResearch Article\nKeywords: Heavy Metals, Coastal Sediment, Risk Assessment, Meta-Analysis, Persian Gulf\nPosted Date: December 20th, 2022\nDOI: https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-2277311/v1\nLicense:   This work is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License.   Read Full\nLicense\nVersion of Record: A version of this preprint was published at Environmental Science and Pollution Research on\nApril 25th, 2023. See the published version at https://doi.org/10.1007/s11356-023-26949-8.\nPage 2/25\nAbstract\nChemical pollution in the Persian Gulf has become an important health concern in recent decades. This\ninvestigation aimed to meta-analyses of potential toxic elements (PTEs) including lead (Pb), inorganic Arsenic\n(As), Cadmium (Cd), Nickel (Ni) and Mercury (Hg) in the coastal sediment of the Persian Gulf. In this study, an\nattempt was made to retrieve papers conducted on the concentration of PTEs in the coastal sediment of the\nPersian Gulf by searching in international databases including Web of Science, Scopus, Embase and PubMed.\nMeta-analysis concentration of PTEs in the coastal sediment of the Persian Gulf was conducted using random\neffects model based on the country subgroup. Additionally, non-dietary risk assessment including non-\ncarcinogenic and carcinogenic risks from ingestion, inhalation and dermal contact routes and ecological risk\nassessment was estimated. Seventy-eight papers with 81 data-reports (1650 sample size) were included in our\nmeta-analysis. The rank order of heavy metals in coastal sediment of the Persian Gulf based on pooled\nconcentration was Ni (65.44 mg/kg) > Pb (58.35 mg/kg) > As (23.78 mg/kg) > Cd (1.75 mg/kg) > Hg (0.77\nmg/kg). The highest concentration of As, Cd, Pb, Ni and Hg was observed in the coastal sediment of Saudi\nArabia, Arab Emirates, Qatar, Iran and Saudi Arabia, respectively. Although the Igeo index in coastal sediment of\nthe Persian gulf was in Grade 1 (Uncontaminated) and grade 2 (slightly contaminated) but total target hazard\nquotient (TTHQ) of adults due to As and Cd and Adolescent due to As, Cd, Pb and Niwas higher than 1 value In\nIran, In Saudi Arabic, TTHQ of adults due to As and Cd and adolescent due to As and Cd was higher than 1\nvalue, In United Arab Emirates, TTHQ of adults due to Cd and Adolescents due to As, Cd and Pb were higher\nthan 1 value and In Qatar, TTHQ of adults age group due to Pb and adolescent age group due to Pb was higher\nthan 1 value. Total cancer risk (TCR) for adults and adolescent due to As was higher than 1E-6 in Iran, United\nArab Emirates and Qatar but TCR for adolescents due to As was higher than 1E-6 in Saudi Arabic. Therefore, it\nis recommended to monitor the concentration of PTEs and implementation of programs to reduce the emission\nof PTEs from resources in the Persian Gulf.\n1. Introduction\nSeveral inuential studies have paid signicant attention to potentially toxic elements (PTEs) because of their\necotoxicity, bioaccumulation, ubiquity, and stability, leading to deleterious consequences on human health and\nthe environment (E Nour et al. 2022, Jeong et al. 2021, Neshat et al. 2023, Ş im ş ek et al. 2022). PTEs are dened\nas natural elements with a relatively high density, at least 5 times higher than water, andtoxic or poisonous\neffects at low concentrations (Witkowska et al. 2021). In addition to classic heavy metals such as copper,\nnickel, cadmium, and zinc, heavy metals also comprise metalloids like arsenic, which can produce in-vivo\ntoxicity even at low exposure levels (Goutam Mukherjee et al. 2022). PTEs are ubiquitous because they occur\nnaturally in the earth's crust. However, there are several anthropogenic sources-including industries,\nmedications, and agricultural practices- in which high concentrations of PTEs can discharge into the\nenvironment (Arfaeinia et al. 2019, Elsagh et al. 2021b).\nIt has been proved that exposure to environmental-related PTEs can endanger human health. For instance,\nmercury is toxic to the nerves and kidneys and is mostly detected in marine and freshwater. Higher mercury\nlevels in the blood can lead to infertility (Díaz de León-Martínez et al. 2022). Lead affects the bone structure\nand impairs nerve cell development and differentiation in children under six years old (Finkelstein et al. 1998,\nWong et al. 2015). One-third of the body's Cadmium (II) is stored in the liver and kidneys. Cadmium's toxicity\nPage 3/25\nmeans that even short-term contact can have serious effects on human health. Kidney damage, insomnia,\nanemia, and cancer of the prostate and bladder are all results of long-term cadmium exposure (Garrett et al.\n2013). All are aware that arsenic is toxic and causes cancer. It exists in the atmosphere, hydrosphere, and soil.\nClaudia et al. proved that there is a relation between newborn birth weight and arsenic in drinking water, such\nthat arsenic-exposed kids lost 57 g of their weight (Hopenhayn et al. 2003, Yang et al. 2003).\nGlobal monitoring of PTEs indicated their proliferation in aquatic ecosystems. The majority of such pollutants\nare sunk into sediments, which are regarded as sensitive indicators for following them in aquatic ecosystems\n(Jeong et al. 2021). PTEs can enter the trophic webs as they are resuspended, or biota in sediments release\nthem into the water column (Kahlon et al. 2018, Ş im ş ek et al. 2022). Additionally, substantial urbanization,\nrapid industrialization, and economic development have signicantly polluted the coastal environments over\nthe past decades, such that releasing a considerable mass of anthropogenic PTEs into coastal environments\nvia a variety of approaches, including riverine intake and atmospheric deposition, has been spotted (Arfaeinia et\nal. 2019, E Nour et al. 2022, Ş im ş ek et al. 2022). For example, Wang et al., (2020) study in coastal sediment of\nEast China sea, the concentration of PTEs (Cu, Zn, Ni, Pb, Cd) was measured coastal East China sea (one of the\nmost developed regions in China). The results shows that coastal sediment of East China sea is unpolluted to\nmoderately polluted. Also, the strong correlations and organic carbon isotope compositions data showed that\nnatural weathering detritus is the main source of pollution in coastal East of China sea(Wang et al. 2020).\nIn the present study, consideration was paid to the Persian Gulf’s sediments. This Gulf is a Mediterranean Sea\nlocated in the Persian Gulf Basin, which is of Cenozoic origin, and linked to the Gulf of Oman by the Strait of\nHormuz. It is a biogeographic sub-province that can be found in the northern Indian Ocean (Elsagh et al.\n2021b). In addition to being one of the world's most vital oil and gas transportation routes, the Persian Gulf is\nalso home to a thriving ecology, and its neighboring countries are rapidly acquiring new beach front\ndevelopments. The leading industries in the surrounding countries are oil and gas reneries; however, the area\nalso hosts several ancillary industries, including the manufacturing of plastic, cement, aluminum, and\npolyethylene piping. Moreover, other routes can harm the sediments along the coasts, such as the discharge of\nwastewater from sh breeding, animal husbandry, agriculture, and municipal solid waste management\n(Arfaeinia et al. 2019, Elsagh et al. 2021b).\nThe concentration of PTEs in the sediments of the Persian Gulf with several lands uses, such as industrial,\nurban, agricultural, and natural areas, were investigated (Arfaeinia et al. 2019). Compared to the natural and\nurban sources, agricultural and industrial elds indicated extremely greater average PTEs content. It was also\ndiscovered that the vast majority of HMs have strong interrelationships, indicating that their release into the\nenvironment likely originated from the same sources (Arfaeinia et al. 2019).\nIn another work, Elsagh et al. just focused on the mean mercury concentration in the sediments of the Persian\nGulf. They proved that human activity was responsible for high mercury concentration in the sediment of six\nsampling regions of the study (3.24 ± 0.28 µg/g) (Elsagh et al. 2021b). Anthropogenic sources such as\ntransporting crude oil and heavy trac, contamination from petroleum compounds, ships' emission of Ballast\nwater, mercury-relating mining, municipal wastewater, and agricultural process were associated with the\nmercury contamination of sediments in the Persian Gulf. Additionally, this catastrophe was assumed to\nbecome even worse by increasing erosion and, consequently, produced sediments containing urban\nPage 4/25\ncontaminants. They claimed that anthropogenic sources and activities caused the movability of mercury in\npolluted sediments (Elsagh et al. 2021b).\nDespite numerous studies in the last two decades in the eld of pollution of Persian Gulf coasts with\nPTEs(Alharbi &El-Sorogy 2017, Chaharlang et al. 2012, Einollahipeer et al. 2013, El-Sorogy et al. 2016, Elsagh et\nal. 2021a, Janadeleh et al. 2018, Karbasdehi et al. 2016, Mahboob et al. 2022, Salimi et al. 2019, Sedigheh et al.\n2021, Vaezi et al. 2015), no study was found in the eld of systematic review-meta-analysis and health risk\nassessment. The main objectives of this study were meta-analysis concentration of PTEs in coastal sediment\nof the Persian Gulf based on the subgroup of countries and estimation of non-dietary risk in adults and\nadolescents age groups and ecological risk assessment.\n2. Method And Material\n2.1. The protocol and search strategy\nThis study was performed for assessing documents relating to the concentration of PTEs\n(As, Cd, Pb, Ni and Hg) in coastal sediment of the Persian Gulf following PRISMA guidelines (Fig. 1) (Atabati et\nal. 2020, Moher et al. 2011). All published investigations in the English language were searched in international\ndatabases including Web of Science, Scopus, Embase, and PubMed as well as Google Scholar (grey literature)\nfrom 1 January 2010 to 10 September 2022. Keywords were selected according to Medical Subject Headings\n(MeSH) and were included\nin international databases: ((ti/ab (\"trace element\") OR ti/ab (\"toxic metals\") OR ti/ab (\"heavy metals\") OR\nti/ab(\"metal(oid)s\") OR ti/ab (\"metals\") OR ti/ab (\"potential toxic metals\") OR ti/ab (\"potential hazard metals\")\nAND ti/ab (\"sediment\") OR ti/ab (\"coastal sediment\") OR ti/ab (\"sea sediment\") OR ti/ab (\"soil\") AND ti/ab\n(\"Persian Gulf\"). In addition, the list of references retrieved papers assessed by authors for the nd missed\npapers.\n2.2. Inclusion and exclusion criteria and extraction of data\nIn the current systematic review study, inclusion and exclusion criteria were assessed by two authors (YA.FA\nand TR.MO) independently based on the title and abstract. Finally, the disagreement between the two authors\nwas resolved by a nal decision of the third author (I.IN). Our criteria for the selection of papers were including\n(a) full-text available of articles, (b) publishing full text in the English language, (c) descriptive study, (d)\nrecording of average with a standard deviation of concentration of PTE and/or range the concentration of PTEs\nin coastal sediment and (e) study conducted on coastal sediment of Persian Gulf. In this regard, interventional\nstudies, case reports, clinical trials, review articles, letters to editors, book chapters and all investigations that\nwere not conducted in the coastal sediment of the Persian Gulf were excluded from our study. Some studies\nthat not presented average concentration and/or range concentration of PTEs in coastal sediment were\nexcluded. The selected data of each study were including country, sample size, average and standard deviation\nof PTEs, contamination factor (CF)and method of detection. The concentration of PTEs including ppb, ppm,\nµg/kg and ng/g was converted to mg/kg.\n2.3. Meta-analysis of the study\nPage 5/25\nThe pooling concentration of PTEs in coastal sediment was meta-analyzed based on average and standard\nerror. SE was calculatedusingbelow equation:\n Eq. (1)\nWhere, N and SD are the number of samples and standard deviation, respectively.\nThe pooled concentration of PTEs in coastal sediment was calculated based on the random effects model\n(REM) in the coastal sediment of the country subgroup. Heterogeneity was detected by I2statistic. I2statistic (I2 \n> 50%) showedhigh heterogeneity.Meta-analysis of data was performed by Stata software, version 14\n(StataCorp, College Station, TX, USA).\n2.4.Non-dietary health risk assessment\nChronic daily intake (mg/kg-d) from ingestion, inhalation and dermal contact routes was estimated using the\nbelow equations(EPA 2000, 2002, 2011):\n Eq. (2)\n Eq. (3)\n Eq. (4)\nWhere, CDIing, CDIder and CDIinh are chronic daily intake doses from ingestion, dermal contact, and inhalation\nroutes.\nThe details of included variables such as C, IRing, EF, ED, BW, SAF, CF, AT, SA, IRinh, ABS, and PEF are presented\nin Appendix1.\nCarcinogenic risk (CR) due to exposure to PTEs was calculated using the below equation (EPA 2000, 2002,\n2011):\n Eq. (5)\nWhere CR is carcinogenic risk; CDI, Chronic daily intake; and CSF, cancer slope factor.\nThe values of CSF were presented in Appendix 2.\nThe non-carcinogenic risk (nCR) due to exposure to PTEs was calculated using below equation:\n Eq. (6)\nWhere, THQ is the target hazard quotient, CDI, is chronic daily intake (mg/kg-d); and RfD, Reference dose\n(mg/kg-d). The value of RfDing, RfDderm and Rfdinh are presented in Appendix 3.\nThe sum non-carcinogenic risk due to ingestion and dermal and inhalation routes were calculated using the\nbelow equation:\nSE = SD\nN0.5\nCDIing = × CF\nCsediment×EF×ED×IRing\nAT×BW\nCDIder = × CFCsediment×SA×SAF×ABS×EF×EDAT×BW\nCDIinh = × CFCsediment×EF×ED×IRinhPEF×AT×BW\nCR = CDI × CSF\nTHQ = CDIRfD\nPage 6/25\n Eq. (7)\nIn this equation, TTHQ is the total target hazard quotient; THQing is, the target hazard quotient of the ingestion\nroute;THQderm, target hazard quotient of the dermal contact route and THQinh, target hazard quotient of the\ninhalation route.\nThe sum carcinogenic risk due to ingestion and dermal and inhalation routes were calculated using the below\nequation:\n Eq. (8)\nIn this equation, TCR is total cancer risk; CRing, cancer risk of ingestion route; CRder, cancer risk of dermal\ncontact route and CRinh, cancer risk of inhalation route.\n2.5. Ecological risk assessment\nGeoaccumulation Index (Igeo)was calculated by the below equation (Barbieri 2016):\n Eq. (9)\nWhere, Igeo is Geoaccumulation Index and CF, contamination factor. Data on CF were extracted from included\npapers (Appendix 4).\n3. Results And Discussion\n3.1. Selection process of papers\nThe number of retrieved papers was equal to 1452, after removing duplicate papers and assessing the title,\nabstract and full text of papers, 78 papers with 81 data-reports (1650 sample size) were included in our meta-\nanalysis (Fig. 1 and Appendix 4).\n3.2. Concentration of heavy metals\nAccording to the results, the rank order of heavy metals in coastal sediment of the Persian Gulf based on\npooled concentration was Ni (65.44 mg/kg, 95% CI (53.85–77.04 mg/kg) > Pb (58.35 mg/kg, 95%CI (51.13–\n65.58 mg/kg) > As (23.78 mg/kg, 95%CI (20.85–26.71 mg/kg) > Cd (1.75 mg/kg, 95%CI (1.62–1.88 mg/kg) > \nHg (0.77 mg/kg, 95%CI (0.70–0.84 mg/kg) (Table 1). The rank order of heavy metal in the coastal sediment of\nIran was Ni > Pb > As > Cd > Hg; in Saudi Arabia, As > Ni > Pb > Cd > Hg; In Qatar Pb > Ni; and in UAE, Pb > Ni > As > \nCd (Fig. 2). As reported from the ndings, the highest concentration of As, Cd, Pb, Ni and Hg were observed in\nthe coastal sediment of the Arab Emirates, Qatar, Iran and Saudi Arabia, respectively (Table 1). In line with our\nstudy, various studies have been conducted in different countries. The mean concentration of heavy metals in\nsediments of our meta-analysis study was compared with those in other regions of the world. For example,\nBibak et al. indicated in an investigation conducted in Iran country, in the sediment of the Persian Gulf mean\nconcentrations of As, Cd, Pb and Ni were 6.5300, 0.1100, 6.1000 and 12.8600 mg/kg, respectively that can be\ndue to transportation activities or oil compounds (Bibak et al. 2018). Haidari et al, Pejman, A. H et al, and\nBastami et al reported mean concentrations of Cd and Pb in the Persian Gulf sediment of Iran country were\nTTHQ = THQing + THQder + THQinh\nTCR = CRing + CRderm + CRinh\nIgeo = log(0.66 × CF)\nPage 7/25\n(1.26,158.86 mg/kg), (0.85,52.24 mg/kg) and (8.10, 721.86 mg/kg), respectively. Their studies stated the\ncontamination of metals in the sediments of this region can be related to various port activities like the\ndumping of cargo ships, shing boats, shipyards, industrial and urban areas activities (Appendix 5)\n(Chaharlang et al. 2012, Pejman et al. 2017). In line with these countries, Mirza et al observed pollution from\ndifferent metals like Cd (0.14 mg/kg), Pb (10.30 mg/kg) and Ni (89.37 mg/kg) in the Persian Gulf sediment of\nIran country can be affected anthropogenic activities as shipping, transportation, transit of oil carriers, shery,\nfuel smuggling, industrial sewage from factories near the study area (Mirzaei et al. 2020). According to studies\nin other countries, Alharbi et al showed mean concentration of As, Cd, Pb, Ni and Hg in the sediment of Saudi\nArabia country was 1.60, 0.22, 5.35, 7510.00 and 0.80 mg/kg respectively. Based on their research, the presence\nof heavy metals in these samples was because of anthropogenic activities, notably oil spills from exploration\nand transportation, saline water desalination plants in the Al-Khobar coast and the industrial activities in the al-\nJubail industrial city to the north (Alharbi et al. 2017). Other studies conducted by Sorogy et al and Youssef et\nal stated mean concentrations of As, and Cd in coastal sediments of, Saudi Arabia country were (0.85, 52.24\nmg/kg) and (121.10, 0.70 mg/kg), respectively that can be due to dredging and landlling, sewage, and oil\npollution that were the most important sources of pollution in the study regions (El-Sorogy et al. 2021). In two\nseparate studies performed by Tokhi et al and Al rashdi et al, in coastal sediments of the Arab Emirates the\ncountry, it was observed that high contamination of metals such as Pb and Ni found in these regions can be\ndependent on oil elds and industrial activities. Ahmed et al indicated mean concentrations of As and Cd in\ncoastal sediments of Saudi Arabia country were (750.0 mg/kg) and (42.70 mg/kg), respectively, which be due\nto sewage euents, industrial discharge, oil activities and harbors. A comparison of the mean concentration of\nheavy metal values revealed that the distribution of As, Cd, Pb, Ni and Hg varied across the locations of\ndifferent countries that may be originated mainly from industrial waste, and human activities (Al Rashdi et al.\n2015, El Nemr et al. 2016a). According to these two key factors, El Nemr et al reported environmental\ncontamination involved in Pb and Cd contamination can be attributed to human activities such as the use of\nCd in batteries, pigments, plating, stabilizers tourism, land-lling, marinas, wastewater discharging (El Nemr et\nal. 2016a) (Appendix 5). In other research, it was determined that anthropogenic environmental sources of Pb\ncan be related to trac exhaust, lead–zinc smelters, dumps and other sites receiving industrial and household\nlead, e.g., paints and batteries (Pejman et al. 2017). Other important parameters inuencing these differences\ncan be mentioned discharge the oil tankers, industrial discharges, agricultural euents, cement factories, trac\nof vehicles, repair shops, petrochemical processes, gas renement, factories producing electronic/electric\npieces, industries for the production of plastic tubes, packaging industries(Appendix 5) (Arisekar et al. 2020,\nBibak et al. 2021).\n3.3. Non-carcinogenic and carcinogenic risk assessment\nThe calculation of TTHQ and total cancer risk (TCR) of adolescents and adults due to heavy metals in the\ncoastal sediment of the Persian Gulf are illustrated in Tables 2–5. Based on results in Iran, TTHQ of the adults’\nage group due to As (12.89E + 00) and Cd (1.23E + 00) and Adolescents age group due to As (8.83E + 00), Cd\n(1.01E + 00), Pb (2.98E + 00) and Ni (1.31E + 00) was higher than 1 value. In Saudi Arabic, TTHQ of the adults’\nage group due to As (1.50E + 01) and Cd (1.01E + 00) and Adolescents age group due to As (4.58E + 01) and Cd\n(3.07E + 00) was higher than 1 value (Table 2). In the United Arab Emirates, TTHQ of the adults’ age group due\nto Cd (1.90E + 00) and Adolescents age group due to As (2.14E + 00), Cd (5.79E + 00) and Pb (2.29E + 00) was\nhigher than 1 value. In Qatar, TTHQ of the adults age group due to Pb (1.51E + 01) and Adolescent age group\nPage 8/25\ndue to Pb (4.61E + 01) was higher than 1 value (Table 3). In Iran, TCR for adults and Adolescents due to As was\nhigher than 1E-6 but in Saudi Arabic TCR for Adolescents due to As was higher than 1E-6 (Table 4). In the\nUnited Arab Emirates, TCR for both adults and Adolescents due to As was higher than 1E-6 and also in Qatar\nTCR for both adults and Adolescents due to As was higher than 1E-6 (Table 5). As mentioned and obtained\nfrom the results, there was a variety of risk patterns that could be caused by the differences in metal\nconcentration in different countries of the region. Moreover, the results of carcinogenic and non-carcinogenic\nrisk of metals in coastal sediment of the Persian Gulf by inhalation and dermal contact were not safe and had\nsome potential risks to human health. As reported by previous studies, exposure to metals can lead to health\nrisks that can be mentioned to disorders in the nervous systems, reproductive and immune systems depression,\nkinds of cancers, anemia, and respiratory complaints (Ghane et al. 2022, Khazaei et al. 2021). In line with our\nresults, various studies have been conducted regarding the risk assessment of metals in different areas Persian\nGulf. El Nemr et al. performed a study about the risk assessment of metals in sediment from the Red Sea coast,\nEgypt. Their ndings indicated calculation of HQ for Ni, Pb, Cd and Hg was between 1 and 10 values. Also\nheavy metals content of ILCR of the mentioned metals obtain 1.0E-032.6E-05, 9.9E-07 and 4.4E-08, respectively;\ntherefore exposure to these metals has health risks (El Nemr et al. 2016b). Al-Mutairi et al. reported in a review\nstudy conducted about metals in coastal surface sediments from the Red Sea, THQ and HI values via ingestion\nand the dermal contact routes of Cd, Cu, Pb, and Zn were found lower of 1, therefore exposure with these\nmetals for both children and adults was without risk to health (Al-Mutairi &Yap 2021).\nDarvish Bastami et al. indicated TTHQ value for the general population at the sediments of the Persian Gulf\nexceeded 1. Therefore, there is a health risk for the residents of these areas in exposure to metals (Bastami et\nal. 2015). In another study, Arisekar et al investigated the human health risk of metals in sediments from the\nThamirabarani River, the Western Ghats of South Tamil Nadu. They found TCR values for Cd metal for children\nand adults were found to be above the acceptable level and maybe poses a high cancer risk to the inhabitants’\nregion (Arisekar et al. 2020). Ş im ş ek et al in research that in order to assess the carcinogenic and non-\ncarcinogenic risks of Ni, Pb and Cd in sediments of Samsun-Tekkeköy, North Turkey country done, their results\nshowed carcinogenic and non-carcinogenic risks were higher among children than adults ( Ş im ş ek et al. 2022).\nAs observed from ndings, anthropogenic and agricultural activities such as transportation services, industrial\nwaste disposal, shing and oil exploration can be considered as possible sources of pollution and high\nconcentration of many heavy metals in different areas and increase the health risk in residents (Mirza et al.\n2019).\n3.4. Ecological risk assessment\nAccording to studies, different indices have been applied to evaluate the contamination of ecosystems. One of\nthe most used indices is the Igeo index. Based on the degree of contamination, this index classies ecosystems\ninto seven groups (from 0 to 6), from uncontaminated to extremely contaminated. In current study, Igeo was\nused to evaluate the contamination degree of As, Cd, Pb, Ni and Hg in the coastal sediment of the Persian Gulf.\nOur results indicated in Iran, Igeo  ≤  0 was related to Ni and As (Grade 1: Uncontaminated) and Igeo 0 < Igeo  ≤  1\nwas related to Hg, Pb and Cd (Grade 2: Slightly contaminated). In Saudi Arabic, Igeo  ≤  0 was related to Ni and\nPb (Grade 1: Uncontaminated) and Igeo 0 < Igeo  ≤  1 was related Hg, As and Cd (Grade 2: Slightly\nPage 9/25\ncontaminated) (Fig. 3 and Appendix 6). Consistent with our evaluation, Alahabadi et al suggested sediments of\nthe Tajan River were non-contaminated with respect to Fe, Ci, Ni and Zn based on the Igeo index. They reported\ncontamination of these areas could be affected by urban and domestic, industrial wastewater, and agricultural\nactivities (Alahabadi &Malvandi 2018). In a similar study, Kadhum et al listed the elements Ni, Cr, and Fe as low\ncontamination in the Bernam River of Malaysia (Kadhum et al. 2016). Liu et al (2021) reported contamination\ndegree of Cd and Pb in sediments of Haizhou Bay, China was all < 0 therefore the southern part of Haizhou Bay\nwas uncontaminated with these heavy metals (Liu et al. 2021). In another study conducted by Li et al (2015) it\nwas observed that the Igeo values of Pb in the sediments of Shuangtaizi estuary was categorized as class 0,\nwhich indicates that the sediments in this region were uncontaminated by Pb metal (Li et al. 2015). Ogundele et\nal showed that overall conditions of the potential ecological risks varied from low to considerable in coastal\nsediments samples of Nigeria which can be related to anthropogenic activities and natural sources that could\ndischarge metals to sediments and contribute to the associated ecological risk (Ogundele &Ayeku 2020).\nFinally, in the present meta-analysis study, although the ecological risk unlike the health risk, was acceptable,\nbut it should be mentioned that in the ecological risk, the effects of metals on the total environment evaluate,\nwhile in the health risk, the effects of metals on human health are considered affected by various parameters,\ntherefore, it should be mentioned that ecological risk alone cannot determine the health conditions of metals in\nthe coastal sediment of the Persian Gulf.\n4. Conclusion\nIn this study, a meta-analysis concentration of PTEs (As, Cd, Pb, Ni and Hg) in coastal sediment of the Persian\nGulf was conducted based on the country subgroup. Additionally, non-dietary risk assessment (non-\ncarcinogenic and carcinogenic risks) and ecological risk were estimated. The rank order of heavy metals in the\ncoastal sediment of the Persian Gulf based on pooled concentration was Ni > Pb > As > Cd > Hg. Although the\necological risk assessment showed that the coastal sediment of the Persian Gulf is uncontaminated or Slightly\ncontaminate but TTHQ for both adults and adolescents due to As, Cd, Pb and Ni was higher than 1 value in\nIran, Saudi Arabic, United Arab Emirates and Qatar. In Iran, United Arab Emirates and Qatar, TCR for adults and\nadolescents due to As was higher than 1E-6 but in Saudi Arabic,TCR for Adolescents due to As was higher than\n1E-6. Therefore, exposed people are at considerable non-dietary health risk. Hence, it is recommended to carry\nout continuous control programs for the concentration of PTEs as well as to identify the sources of PTEs of\ncoastal and try to reduce the emission of PTEs into the coastal sediments of the Persian Gulf.\nDeclarations\nEthics approval\nNot applicable\nAvailability of data and materials\nNot applicable\nConict of interest\nPage 10/25\nThe authors declare no conict of interest relevant to this article.\nConsent to Participate\nThe authors declare their consent to participate in this article.\nConsent to Publish\nThe authors declare their Consent to Publish this article.\nAuthors contributions \nXiaomei Hui, Yadolah Fakhri and Van Nam Thai: Supervision, Investigation, Resources, Conceptualization, Risk\nAssessment, Methodology, Writing original draft, Yadolah Fakhri, Amir Mohammad Sheikh Asadi: Literature\nSearching, Writing & Editing, Fereshteh Mehri and Intissar Limam: Writing & Editing, Review & Editing, Xiaomei\nHui and Van Nam Thai  Yadolah Fakhri: Meta-Analysis of data, Supervision, Risk Assessment, Review &\nEditing. \nAcknowledgment\nNot applicable\nFunding \nNot applicable\nReferences\n1. Al-Mutairi KA, Yap CK (2021): A review of heavy metals in coastal surface sediments from the red sea:\nHealth-ecological risk assessments. International journal of environmental research and public health 18,\n2798\n2. Al Rashdi S, Arabi AA, Howari FM, Siad A (2015): Distribution of heavy metals in the coastal area of Abu\nDhabi in the United Arab Emirates. Mar. Pollut. Bull. 97, 494-498\n3. Alahabadi A, Malvandi H (2018): Contamination and ecological risk assessment of heavy metals and\nmetalloids in surface sediments of the Tajan River, Iran. Marine Pollution Bulletin 133, 741-749\n4. Alharbi T, Alfai H, Almadani SA, El-Sorogy A (2017): Spatial distribution and metal contamination in the\ncoastal sediments of Al-Khafji area, Arabian Gulf, Saudi Arabia. Environmental monitoring and assessment\n189, 1-15\n5. Alharbi T, El-Sorogy A (2017): Assessment of metal contamination in coastal sediments of Al-Khobar area,\nArabian Gulf, Saudi Arabia. Journal of African Earth Sciences 129, 458-468\n. Arfaeinia H, Dobaradaran S, Moradi M, Pasalari H, Mehrizi EA, Taghizadeh F, Esmaili A, Ansarizadeh M\n(2019): The effect of land use congurations on concentration, spatial distribution, and ecological risk of\nheavy metals in coastal sediments of northern part along the Persian Gulf. Science of The Total\nEnvironment 653, 783-791\nPage 11/25\n7. Arisekar U, Shakila RJ, Shalini R, Jeyasekaran G (2020): Human health risk assessment of heavy metals in\naquatic sediments and freshwater sh caught from Thamirabarani River, the Western Ghats of South\nTamil Nadu. Marine Pollution Bulletin 159, 111496\n. Atabati H, Abouhamzeh B, Abdollahifar M-A, Javadinia SS, Bajestani SG, Atamaleki A, Raoo A, Fakhri Y,\nOliveira CA, Khaneghah AM (2020): The association between high oral intake of acrylamide and risk of\nbreast cancer: an updated systematic review and meta-analysis. Trends in Food Science & Technology\n100, 155-163\n9. Barbieri M (2016): The importance of enrichment factor (EF) and geoaccumulation index (Igeo) to evaluate\nthe soil contamination. J Geol Geophys 5, 1-4\n10. Bastami KD, Afkhami M, Mohammadizadeh M, Ehsanpour M, Chambari S, Aghaei S, Esmaeilzadeh M,\nNeyestani MR, Lagzaee F, Baniamam M (2015): Bioaccumulation and ecological risk assessment of heavy\nmetals in the sediments and mullet Liza klunzingeri in the northern part of the Persian Gulf. Marine\npollution bulletin 94, 329-334\n11. Bibak M, Sattari M, Agharokh A, Tahmasebi S, Imanpour Namin J (2018): Assessing some heavy metals\npollutions in sediments of the northern Persian Gulf (Bushehr province). ,5      \n175-179\n12. Bibak M, Sattari M, Tahmasebi S, Kafaei R, Sorial GA, Ramavandi B (2021): Trace and major elements\nconcentration in sh and associated sediment–seawater, northern shores of the Persian Gulf. Biological\nTrace Element Research 199, 2717-2729\n13. Chaharlang BH, Bakhtiari AR, Yavari V (2012): Assessment of cadmium, copper, lead and zinc\ncontamination using oysters (Saccostrea cucullata) as biomonitors on the coast of the Persian Gulf, Iran.\nBulletin of environmental contamination and toxicology 88, 956-961\n14. Díaz de León-Martínez L, Ortega-Romero M, Gavilán-García A, Barbier OC, Carrizalez-Yáñez L, Van-Brusel E,\nDíaz-Barriga F, Flores-Ramírez R (2022): Assessment of biomarkers of early kidney damage and exposure\nto pollutants in artisanal mercury mining workers from Mexico. Environmental Science and Pollution\nResearch 29, 13333-13343\n15. E Nour H, Alshehri F, Sahour H, El-Sorogy AS, Tawk M (2022): Assessment of heavy metal contamination\nand health risk in the coastal sediments of Suez Bay, Gulf of Suez, Egypt. Journal of African Earth\nSciences 195, 104663\n1. Einollahipeer F, Khammar S, Sabaghzadeh A (2013): A study on heavy metal concentration in sediment\nand mangrove (Avicenia marina) tissues in Qeshm island, Persian Gulf. Journal of Novel Applied Sciences\n2, 498-504\n17. El-Sorogy AS, Youssef M, Al-Kahtany K, Al-Otaiby N (2016): Assessment of arsenic in coastal sediments,\nseawaters and molluscs in the Tarut Island, Arabian Gulf, Saudi Arabia. J. Afr. Earth Sci. 113, 65-72\n1. El-Sorogy AS, Youssef M, Al-Kahtany K (2021): Evaluation of coastal sediments for heavy metal\ncontamination, Yanbu area, Red Sea coast, Saudi Arabia. Marine Pollution Bulletin 163, 111966\n19. El Nemr A, El-Said GF, Khaled A, Ragab S (2016a): Distribution and ecological risk assessment of some\nheavy metals in coastal surface sediments along the Red Sea, Egypt. International Journal of Sediment\nResearch 31, 164-172\nPage 12/25\n20. El Nemr A, El-Said GF, Ragab S, Khaled A, El-Sikaily A (2016b): The distribution, contamination and risk\nassessment of heavy metals in sediment and shellsh from the Red Sea coast, Egypt. Chemosphere 165,\n369-380\n21. Elsagh A, Jalilian H, Aslshabestari MG (2021a): Evaluation of heavy metal pollution in coastal sediments\nof Bandar Abbas, the Persian Gulf, Iran: Mercury pollution and environmental geochemical indices. Mar.\nPollut. Bull. 167, 112314\n22. Elsagh A, Jalilian H, Ghaderi Aslshabestari M (2021b): Evaluation of heavy metal pollution in coastal\nsediments of Bandar Abbas, the Persian Gulf, Iran: Mercury pollution and environmental geochemical\nindices. Marine Pollution Bulletin 167, 112314\n23. EPA (2000): United States Environmental Protection Agency (U.S. EPA) (2000). Risk characterization\nhandbook. EPA 100-B-00-002. Washington, DC.\n24. EPA (2002): Supplemental guidance for developing soil screening levels for superfund sites. Oce of Solid\nWaste and Emergency Response, Washington, D.C.\nhttp://www.epa.gov/superfund/health/conmedia/soil/index.htm. 5-9\n25. EPA (2011): Exposure Factors Handbook - Chapter 15: Human Milk Intake. https://ofmpub.epa.gov › eims ›\neimscomm.getle, 5, 7-15 pp\n2. Finkelstein Y, Markowitz ME, Rosen JF (1998): Low-level lead-induced neurotoxicity in children: an update\non central nervous system effects. Brain Research Reviews 27, 168-176\n27. Garrett SH, Clarke K, Sens DA, Deng Y, Somji S, Zhang KK (2013): Short and long term gene expression\nvariation and networking in human proximal tubule cells when exposed to cadmium. BMC Medical\nGenomics 6, S2\n2. Ghane ET, Poormohammadi A, Khazaei S, Mehri F (2022): Concentration of potentially toxic elements in\nvegetable oils and health risk assessment: a systematic review and meta-analysis. Biological Trace\nElement Research 200, 437-446\n29. Goutam Mukherjee A, Ramesh Wanjari U, Renu K, Vellingiri B, Valsala Gopalakrishnan A (2022): Heavy\nmetal and metalloid - induced reproductive toxicity. Environmental Toxicology and Pharmacology 92,\n103859\n30. Hopenhayn C, Ferreccio C, Browning SR, Huang B, Peralta C, Gibb H, Hertz-Picciotto I (2003): Arsenic\nExposure from Drinking Water and Birth Weight. Epidemiology 14\n31. Janadeleh H, Jahangiri S, Kameli MA (2018): Assessment of heavy metal pollution and ecological risk in\nmarine sediments (A case study: Persian Gulf). Human and Ecological Risk Assessment: An International\nJournal 24, 2265-2274\n32. Jeong H, Choi JY, Choi D-H, Noh J-H, Ra K (2021): Heavy metal pollution assessment in coastal sediments\nand bioaccumulation on seagrass (Enhalus acoroides) of Palau. Marine Pollution Bulletin 163, 111912\n33. Kadhum SA, Ishak MY, Zulkii SZ (2016): Evaluation and assessment of baseline metal contamination in\nsurface sediments from the Bernam River, Malaysia. Environmental Science and Pollution Research 23,\n6312-6321\n34. Kahlon SK, Sharma G, Julka JM, Kumar A, Sharma S, Stadler FJ (2018): Impact of heavy metals and\nnanoparticles on aquatic biota. Environmental Chemistry Letters 16, 919-946\nPage 13/25\n35. Karbasdehi VN, Dobaradaran S, Nabipour I, Ostovar A, Vazirizadeh A, Ravanipour M, Nazmara S, Keshtkar\nM, Mirahmadi R, Noorinezhad M (2016): A new bioindicator, shell of Trachycardium lacunosum, and\nsediment samples to monitors metals (Al, Zn, Fe, Mn, Ni, V, Co, Cr and Cu) in marine environment: The\nPersian Gulf as a case. Journal of Environmental Health Science and Engineering 14, 1-12\n3. Khazaei S, Talebi Ghane E, Bashirian S, Mehri F (2021): The concentration of potentially toxic elements\n(PTEs) in fruit juices: a global systematic review, meta-analysis and probabilistic health risk assessment.\nInternational Journal of Environmental Analytical Chemistry, 1-13\n37. Li C, Song C, Yin Y, Sun M, Tao P, Shao M (2015): Spatial distribution and risk assessment of heavy metals\nin sediments of Shuangtaizi estuary, China. Mar. Pollut. Bull. 98, 358-364\n3. Liu B, Xu M, Wang J, Wang Z, Zhao L (2021): Ecological risk assessment and heavy metal contamination\nin the surface sediments of Haizhou Bay, China. Mar. Pollut. Bull. 163, 111954\n39. Mahboob S, Ahmed Z, Khan MF, Virik P, Al-Mulhm N, Baabbad AA (2022): Assessment of heavy metals\npollution in seawater and sediments in the Arabian Gulf, near Dammam, Saudi Arabia. Journal of King\nSaud University-Science 34, 101677\n40. Mirza R, Moeinaddini M, Pourebrahim S, Zahed MA (2019): Contamination, ecological risk and source\nidentication of metals by multivariate analysis in surface sediments of the khouran Straits, the Persian\nGulf. Marine pollution bulletin 145, 526-535\n41. Mirzaei M, Hatamimanesh M, Haghshenas A, Moghaddam SM, Ozunu A, Azadi H (2020): Spatial-seasonal\nvariations and ecological risk of heavy metals in Persian gulf coastal region: case study of Iran. J. Enviorn.\nHealth Sci. Eng. 18, 91-105\n42. Moher D, Altman DG, Liberati A, Tetzlaff J (2011): PRISMA statement. Epidemiology 22, 128\n43. Neshat A, Oghazyan A, Kariminejad F, Mahmudiono T, Fakhri Y, Sheikh Asadi AM, Atamaleki A, Mousavi\nKhaneghah A (2023): The concentration of potentially toxic elements (PTEs) in human milk: A systematic\nreview, meta-analysis, and health risk assessment. Journal of Food Composition and Analysis 115, 104933\n44. Ogundele LT, Ayeku PO (2020): Source apportionment and associated potential ecological risk assessment\nof heavy metals in coastal marine sediments samples in Ondo, Southwest, Nigeria. Stochastic\nEnvironmental Research and Risk Assessment 34, 2013-2022\n45. Pejman A, Bidhendi GN, Ardestani M, Saeedi M, Baghvand A (2017): Fractionation of heavy metals in\nsediments and assessment of their availability risk: A case study in the northwestern of Persian Gulf