 {
  "title": "Evaluation of the disinfection effect and mechanism of SO4•− and HO• UV/persulfate salts",
  "url": "https://doi.org/10.1007/s11356-023-26120-3",
  "source": "Environmental Science and Pollution Research",
  "journal": "Environmental Science and Pollution Research",
  "journal_tier": "top",
  "date": "2023-02-25",
  "category": "literature",
  "department": "其他",
  "region": "国际",
  "quality": "C",
  "summary": "Evaluation of the disinfection effect and mechanism of SO4•− and HO• UV/persulfate salts",
  "tags": [],
  "content_fetched": true,
  "pdf": "kb/pdf/fb2a78137ac05a21.pdf",
  "cid": "fb2a78137ac05a21",
  "importance": 5.59,
  "added_at": "2026-10-06",
  "_ev": "ti:398be77ce51ab104",
  "quality_src": "B",
  "water_rel": 0.3,
  "kg_terms": [],
  "content_type": "研究文献",
  "kclass": "research",
  "content_class": "academic",
  "gate_status": "keep",
  "gate_reason": "学术文献（top：Environmental Science and Pollution Research）",
  "content": "Page 1/21\nEvaluation of the disinfection effect and mechanism\nof SO4•- and HO• in UV / persulfate salts from the\nperspective of VBNC bacteria\nZaihui Huang \nShandong University\nZheng Qi \nShandong University\nChunguang Liu  (  chunguangliu2013@sdu.edu.cn )\nShandong University\nResearch Article\nKeywords: Viable but nonculturable cells, Sulfate radicals, Bacterial metabolism, Persulfate salts, HO•\nradicals\nPosted Date: October 5th, 2022\nDOI: https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-1803755/v1\nLicense:   This work is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License.  \nRead Full License\nVersion of Record: A version of this preprint was published at Environmental Science and Pollution\nResearch on February 25th, 2023. See the published version at https://doi.org/10.1007/s11356-023-\n26120-3.\nPage 2/21\nAbstract\nAlthough ultraviolet (UV) and persulfate (PS) have been widely used in water disinfection process, their\nincompleteness of disinfection, such as inducing the production of viable but non-culturable cells (VBNC),\nhas attracted extensive attention. In this study, the disinfection effect of combined UV and PS was\nevaluated from the perspective of inducing Pseudomonas aeruginosa into VBNC, and the roles of SO4•−\nand HO• radicals in UV/PS disinfection were also analyzed. UV/PS more effectively inactivated cells and\nreduced the number of culturable cells, but induced more VBNC cells. On the other hand, the test of\nbacterial dark activation suggested that UV/PS disinfection effectively prolongs the recovery time of\nVBNC cells. The mechanisms of UV/PS disinfection were the increase of membrane permeability and\noxidative stress, where SO4•− radicals played more role than HO• radicals. Furthermore, UV/PS\ndisinfection more signicantly perturbed the metabolism of Pseudomonas aeruginosa (p < 0.05), mainly\ninvolving glyoxylate and dicarboxylic acid metabolism, aminoacyl-tRNA biosynthesis, Alanine, aspartate\nand glutamate metabolism, citric acid cycle (TCA cycle). This study, from the production and recovery of\nVBNC, provides a new idea for the evaluation of UV, persulfate and their combined disinfection effect, and\nprovides guidance for improving disinfection process.\n1. Introduction\nDisinfection is critical for inactivating pathogens and preventing waterborne diseases, but the production\nof viable but non-culturable cells (VBNC) during the disinfection process cause potential ecological risks\nand health hazards(Achouri et al. 2019), such as ultraviolet (UV) radiation and chlorine disinfection (Chen\net al. 2018, Zhang et al. 2015). In recent years, persulfate (PS) has been widely used in water restoration\nand has achieved promising results for water treatment (Bianco et al. 2017, Marjanovic et al. 2018).\nCompared with chlorine disinfection, persulfate disinfection can be activated to generate free radicals\nwith high oxidation potential. Sulfate radicals have strong oxidizing power and are more selective\noxidants than HO• (Liu et al. 2022, Lutze et al. 2015). Methods of persulfate excitation to produce free\nradicals comprise such as radiation, homogeneous and heterogeneous catalysis, heat, or ultrasound\n(Zhang et al. 2022). Compared to Fe (II), the activation of PS by UV radiation is a more eco-friendly\nmethod for the generation of radicals (Wang et al. 2019), in which SO4•− and HO• were the main radicals\n(Kaur et al. 2020), and have been demonstrated to play an important role in degrading pollutants and\ninactivating resistant bacteria (De Luca et al. 2017, Hong et al. 2017, Moreno-Andres et al. 2019).\nUV disinfection primarily targets intracellular nucleic acids and thus generally preserves cellular integrity\n(Bui et al. 2022, Zheng et al. 2021b). Compared with hydroxyl radical (HO•), activated persulfate can\ngenerate more selective and stable sulfate radical (SO4•−) with longer half-life (Moreno-Andres et al. 2019,\nZhuang et al. 2015). Therefore, the role of SO4•− and HO• in UV/PS disinfection is studied in this research.\nS2O82− + UV → 2SO∙−4\nPage 3/21\nHeterotrophic plate count (HPC) is a frequently used method for determining whether P. aeruginosa has\nbeen inactivated after disinfection (Pousty et al. 2021, Tran et al. 2022). However, HPC is unable to detect\npathogens in VBNC state that are still metabolically active. These VBNC cells can be \"revived\" under\nenvironmentally stimulating or permissive growth conditions, restoring their ability to grow (Campo et al.\n2021, Fu et al. 2021). When 5-cyano-2,3-ditolyl tetrazolium chloride (CTC) staining is combined with ow\ncytometry (FCM), metabolically active cells can be rapidly and accurately detected (Lin et al. 2017,\nWilkinson 2018). Using heavy water (D2O) stable isotope tracing combined with Raman microscopy can\nidentify and sort active microorganisms at the single-cell level of complex samples (Berry et al. 2015).\nCellular metabolic activity can be assessed by detecting the formation of C-D bonds during fatty acid and\nprotein synthesis (Guo et al. 2019). Metabolomics enables the quantitative analysis of all metabolites in\nan organism, providing information about overall changes in biochemical pathways(Lisec et al. 2006).\nTherefore, this research evaluated the disinfection effect of P. aeruginosa by UV/PS through CTC-FCM,\nHPC, GC-MS/MS, and D2O-labeled single-cell Raman spectroscopy methods from the perspective of\nVBNC.\nSo far, the disinfection mechanism of the advanced oxidation of PS has not yet been claried. Most\nresearch results indicate that it is related to the integrity of the bacterial cell structure and the leakage of\nintracellular functional substances (Wang et al. 2020). Free radicals (SO4•− and HO•) can damage the\nintegrity of the membrane and cause the leakage of intracellular substances. Membrane lipid\nperoxidation changes the physical function of the lipid bilayer, affecting the uidity and permeability of\nthe membrane (Gaschler &Stockwell 2017). Reactive oxygen species (ROS) can affect metabolism,\noxidative stress, and signal transduction. Superoxide Dismutase (SOD) is the rst line of defense against\nactive oxygen (Apel &Hirt 2004). The degree of oxidative stress in the cell depends on the amount of\nsuperoxide, hydrogen peroxide, and hydroxyl free radicals (Sies et al. 2017). Therefore, this article\nevaluates the disinfection mechanism of UV/PS by investigating cell membrane permeability,\nMalondialdehyde (MDA), Catalase (CAT), and Superoxide Dismutase (SOD).\nThe purpose of this study is to understand the effect and mechanism of UV/PS disinfection from the\nperspective of VBNC bacteria. P. aeruginosa is famous for its wide distribution and conditional\npathogenicity in the environment, so it was selected as the target microorganism of this study (Martegani\net al. 2020). The cultivability, metabolic activity, and metabolic pathways of P. aeruginosa after\ndisinfection were evaluated. In addition, the disinfection mechanism of UV/PS was investigated from the\naspects of membrane permeability and oxidative stress. Potential environmental risks that may be\nassociated with UV/PS disinfection are also assessed.\n2 Materials And Methods\n2.1 Bacterial strain and Chemicals\nThe gram-negative bacterium P. aeruginosa [CMCC10104] freeze-dried powder was purchased from\nNanjing Clinic Biotechnology Company. The Reactive oxygen species Assay Kit was purchased from Med\nPage 4/21\nChem Express USA. Malondialdehyde (MDA), Superoxide Dismutase (SOD), Catalase (CAT) and Inhibition\nand produce superoxide anion assay kit were purchased from Nanjing Jiancheng Institute of\nBioengineering. Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. provided the sodium persulfate and other\nsubstances used in this study.\n2.2 Cell culture\nThe lyophilized powder of P. aeruginosa was dissolved in 100 mL of sterile LB medium. Then, the\nbacteria (OD600 = 0.2 ~ 0.8) were inoculated into 1000 mL of sterile medium at an inoculum of 1% (v/v),\nand for shaking culture at 37°C overnight. After that, the bacteria were centrifuged at 3000 × g for 10 min\nat room temperature and washed twice with 0.9% saline to remove the culture medium for subsequent\nexperiments.\n2.3 Experimental Procedure\nThe bacterial solution (OD600 = 0.17 ~ 0.18) was placed in a quartz container, and then a certain volume\nof sodium persulfate solution was added to a concentration of 1 mM, followed by UV light irradiation (15\nW, 254 nm). Bacterial characteristics after 60 min of treatment were analyzed.\n2.4 GC-MS/MS to test the effect of UV/PS on cell metabolic\npathways\nThe pretreatment of GC-MS/MS is an improvement on the method of Lisec et al. (2006). Specically, after\nthe bacteria were exposed and crushed (SCIENTZ, Ningbo), 1.4 mL of 100% methanol was added to the\nbacterial suspension, mixed and centrifuged (11,000 × g, 10 min) followed by 750 µL of chloroform and\ncentrifuged again for 10 min (220 × g). After freezing dried, 40 µL of methoxyamine hydrochloride and 70\nµL of N-methyl-N-(trimethylsilyl) triuoroacetamide were sequentially added, and derivatized at 37°C for 2\nh and 30 min in the dark, respectively. Then, 410 µL of ethyl acetate was added for analysis.\n2.5 Detection of cell membrane integrity\n2.5.1 Measurement of membrane permeability\nAccording to the method of Helander and Mattila-Sandholm (2020), 200 µL of the bacterial suspension\nafter exposure was added to a 96-well plate and mixed with 1.5 µL of 100 mM NPN. When the cell\nmembrane is damaged, NPN will enter the phospholipid layer, which will promote uorescence. The\nuorescence value was immediately measured with a microplate reader ((TECAN SPARK, USA) at\nexcitation and emission wavelengths of 350 and 420 nm, respectively. Denote the untreated group as \"A\"\nand the experimental group as \"B\". Therefore, the relative Fluorescence intensity is dened as follows:\nRelative Fluorescence intensity = A/B\n2.5.2 Lipid peroxidation\nPage 5/21\nAfter exposure, the bacteria were disrupted by an ultrasonic cell disruptor (300 w, ice water bath). Follow\nthe instructions provided with the kit for testing. Use OD600 to normalize the nal experimental results.\n2.6 Determination of metabolic activity in single cells\nD2O was added after mixing the LB broth and post-exposure bacterial solution to obtain the nal 50%\n(v/v) D2O concentration. Incubate the sample in the dark at 37°C for at least 12 hours to allow P.\naeruginosa to absorb D2O. To remove the impact of the medium, the collected samples were washed 3\ntimes with sterile water and centrifuged at 8000 × g for 5 min. Finally, 10 µL of the prepared solution was\ndropped on the microscope slide and air-dried for measurement before Raman analysis (Thermo DXR2,\nUSA).\n2.7 Determination of ROS, SOD and CAT\nROS, SOD and CAT were measured using ROS, SOD and CAT kit. After exposure, the bacteria were\nresuspended in 5mL of normal saline by centrifugation and then disrupted by a sonicator (300 w, ice\nwater bath). Follow the instructions provided with the kit for testing. Use OD600 to normalize the nal\nexperimental results. Denote the untreated group as \"A\" and the experimental group as \"B\". Therefore, the\nrelative Fluorescence intensity is dened as follows:\nRelative Fluorescence intensity = A/B\n2.8 Resuscitation experiment\nBacterial resuscitation ability was measured in 96-well microplates, using OD600 as the standard to\ndetermine the growth of bacteria (Wang et al. 2021a, Zhang et al. 2015). Simply, the bacteria treated in\ndifferent ways were diluted according to the previous culture results to ensure that the bacteria are all in\nthe VBNC state. Fill each well with 100 µL of diluted bacterial solution and 100 µL of LB broth, and place\nit in the dark at 37°C for shaking culture. The absorbance at 600 nm was measured by a microplate\nreader on days 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, and 9.\n2.9 Statistical analysis\nAll experimental treatments were carried out in triplicate, and the results were expressed as mean\nstandard deviation. The data were analyzed by one-way ANOVA. Use lowercase letters to indicate\nsignicant differences (P < 0.05), those with the same letters indicate no signicant differences between\ngroups, and those without the same letters indicate signicant differences between groups.\n3 Results And Discussions\n3.1 Disinfection effect of Ultraviolet stimulated persulfate\nFigure 1 depicts the number of cultivable P. aeruginosa cells after 60 minutes of UV, PS, UV/PS, and\nUV/H2O2 disinfection. UV/PS disinfection inactivates 2.9-log10 cultivable bacteria within 60 min, while the\nPage 6/21\ncultivable bacteria decrease by 0.9-log10 after 60 min of UV disinfection. Activated bacteria are counted\nusing FCM combined with CTC. Experimental data demonstrated that UV/PS disinfection also exhibited\nbetter results in inactivating cells than either UV or PS alone, suggesting the important role of HO• and\nSO4•− (Tsitonaki et al. 2010). SO4•− is an electrophilic reagent, SO4•− is more prone to electron transfer\nreaction than HO•, while HO• is more readily undergoes hydrogen abstraction or addition (Liu et al. 2021).\nThe different reaction mechanisms of the two highly active substances can act synergistically to\nenhance the disinfection effect.\nMethanol (MeOH) and tert-butanol (t-BuOH) as radical scavengers were used to further analyze the effect\nof radicals (Fig. 1). MeOH can react with HO• (9.7×108mol−1s− 1) and SO4•− (3.2×106 mol− 1s− 1), In\naddition, t-BuOH can react with HO• (6.0×108 mol− 1s− 1), which has a much higher reaction rate than\nSO4•− (Zhou et al. 2020). Therefore, MeOH is mainly used to quench SO4•− and HO•, t-BuOH is mainly\nused to quench HO•. For culturable cells, either adding MeOH or t-BuOH increased the number of\nculturable cells, indicating that both SO4•− and HO• played a very important role. Compared with UV/PS,\nthe addition of MeOH increased the number of cultivable bacteria by 0.2-log10, while the addition of t-\nBuOH had no signicant effect on the number of cultivable bacteria, only an increase of 0.01-log10. This\nresult also indicated that SO4•− played a more important role in effectively reducing culturable cells in\nUV/PS system.\n3.2 Survival of VBNC Cells in UV, PS, and UV/PS\ndisinfection\nThe viable but non-culturable cells (VBNC) are dened by combining HPC and FCM assay results in this\nstudy (Fig. 1). Figure 1 shows that all disinfection treatments induce bacteria to enter the VBNC state in\nvarying degrees. Among them, UV can make bacteria enter the VBNC state, which is the same as the\nprevious conclusions (Zhang et al. 2015), while UV/PS and UV/H2O2 disinfection resulted in a signicant\nincrease in the proportion of VBNC was unexpected. This difference in disinfection may be caused by\ndifferent free radicals. The cell viability of bacteria was examined after UV/PS and UV/H2O2 disinfection\nat the same oxidant concentration of 1 mM to further investigate the functions of HO• and SO4•− in\nUV/PS disinfection. P. aeruginosa cultivable bacteria after UV/PS and UV/H2O2 disinfection were 1.91-\nlog10 and 1.87-log10, respectively, while VBNC cells were 2.41-log10 (UV/H2O2) and 2.31-log10 (UV/PS).\nThe degradation results of Nitrobenzene (NB) showed that the HO• concentration measured in the UV/PS\nprocess is lower than that in the UV/H2O2 process at the same oxidant concentration of 1 mM (Fig. S2).\nAll these results suggest that HO• may play an important role in the induction of VBNC cells compared\nwith SO4•−. Unfortunately, although UV/PS has advantages in both bactericidal ability and reduced\ncultural capacity, UV/PS increases the relative abundance of VBNC, which is more likely to lead to\npotential risks.\n3.3 Assessing the resurrection risk of VBNC\nPage 7/21\nFigure 2 depicts P. aeruginosa reactivation after 60 minutes of UV, PS, and UV/PS disinfection. The\nbacteria in the control group quickly reach logarithmic phase (OD600 = 0.8 ~ 0.9) on the rst day (Fig. 2a).\nHowever, UV group and UV/PS group show the opposite situation that OD600 of bacteria only reach 0.4 \n~ 0.5 on the rst day, which shows that both UV group and UV/PS disinfection have good bactericidal\neffects (inducing bacteria to enter VBNC or killing bacteria). The difference is that persulfate shows\nsustained antibacterial effect not bactericidal effect (Fig. 2c). In the process of recovery, the VBNC\nbacteria induced by UV and UV/PS groups begin to recover from the rst day, but the recovery of VBNC\nbacteria induced by UV/PS group is slower (Fig. 2d), and the total number of bacteria recovered is one\norder of magnitude less than that treated by UV. Therefore, UV/PS has better bactericidal effect than\nsingle UV disinfection in inhibiting resuscitation and bacterial death.\nTo further determine the metabolic activity of the bacteria within 1–2 d, single-cell Raman assays were\nperformed on the bacteria after re-culturing for 36 h. Figure S3a depicts the C-D/(C-D + C-H) ratios, which\nrepresent P. aeruginosa single-cell metabolic activity after 60 min of UV, PS, and UV/PS disinfection. The\nC-D/(C-D + C-H) ratio of bacteria remains relatively low (35%) after 36 h of recultivation, and increases to\n36.2% after UV/PS disinfection, which indicates that cells still keep the ability to continue to absorb\nnutrients after UV/PS disinfection. Figure S3b shows that C-H + C-D in the blank group is second only to\nthe UV/PS group, and the decrease in the proportion is the uptake of C-D by the cells. Metabolic activity\nbased on substrate uptake involves complex active pathways, including transcription, translation, energy\ndependence, and enzyme activity (Ye et al. 2019, Ye et al. 2017).\nThe results of cell recovery experiments and Raman data showed that UV/PS cells had higher cell\nviability after 36 h of re-cultivation, but the total bacterial count was lower than that of the UV group.\nSublethal injury cells can be quickly recovered when they are re-cultured in an appropriate medium and\ntemperature, and regain their normal physiological and biochemical properties (Nasi ł owska et al. 2019).\nHowever, the main difference between sublethal injury cells and VBNC cells is that the cell membrane of\nsublethal injured cells is damaged and more sensitive to external stimuli(Li et al. 2017). Through\nresuscitation experiments, Raman data, and the analysis of membrane permeability and lipid\nperoxidation degree in 3.3, it can be speculated that UV/PS disinfection is more likely to cause sublethal\ndamage to cells.\n3.4 Membrane integrity of cells after UV, PS, UV/PS\ndisinfection\nThe permeability of the cell membrane reects the integrity of the bacterial cell membrane to a certain\nextent (Zheng et al. 2021a). Different disinfections damage the integrity of cell membranes (Fig. 3a). The\ndye permeability of bacteria in the UV/PS disinfection group signicantly increased by 167.75%, which\nmeans that the cell membrane has been severely damaged. The dye permeability of bacteria in the UV\ndisinfection group and PS disinfection group increased by 132.46% and 66.08%, respectively. The cell\nmembrane, as the rst barrier, could maintain a stable metabolic intracellular environment. Once the cell\nmembrane is disrupted, the entire bacterial metabolic system falls apart (Liu et al. 2019). In order to\nPage 8/21\nfurther conrm the role of the two main free radicals in UV/PS system, UV/H2O2 and UV/PS groups with\nMeOH and t-BuOH were set up, respectively. The low cell permeability of UV/H2O2 indicates that HO• has\nno signicant effect on cell membranes. After stopping the action of HO• at the same time, it was found\nthat the permeability did not signicantly decrease, and after stopping the action of SO4•− and HO• at the\nsame time, the permeability of the cell membrane decreased by 60%. In short, SO4•− has a greater effect\non the permeability of cell membranes than HO•.\nTo further clarify the mechanism of cell membranes, we tested the degree of lipid peroxidation and\nprotein content (Fig, 3b and Fig. S4). As the main component of cellular membranes, lipids have an\nindispensable role in maintaining the structural integrity of cells. Like the results of cell permeability, the\ndegree of lipid peroxidation of the cell membrane also increases. Specically, in the experimental group,\nlipid damage by UV and UV/PS increased by 28.91% and 43.10%, respectively. Meanwhile, among\nbacterial metabolites (Table 1), linoleic acid was only detected in the control group, and the other groups\nare all below the detection line. These results indicate that different disinfection methods can cause\ndifferent degrees of oxidative damage to lipids. As shown in Figure S3, the protein content of UV/PS\ndisinfection decreased signicantly. The damage of both structural protein and functional protein will\ncause the loss of normal physiological function of cells. The dual destruction of lipids and proteins by\nUV/PS is the main cause of membrane perforation, which lays the foundation for the continuous action\nof subsequent free radicals.\nTable 1\nThe relative content of non-enzymatic antioxidants\nMetabolite Control UV UV/PS PS\nLinoleic acid 100 -3 -3 -3\nGlycine 2.5 34 / 63\nL-Proline 50 12 11 27\nL-Ascorbic acid 14 19 22 45\nL-Glutamate / 100 / /\nL-Cysteine / / 45 55\n5-Oxoproline 100 / / /\nSalicylic acid 100 / / /\n3.5 Response of P. aeruginosa to oxidative stress\nROS is very important for cell signaling, but excessive ROS can cause genome instability and damage\nlipids, proteins, and deoxyribonucleic acid (Wang et al. 2017). As shown in Fig. 4a, compared with the\ncontrol group, the level of ROS increased by 22.8% and 41.4% after UV and UV/PS disinfection,\nrespectively. In the oxidative stress system, a UV/H2O2 experimental group was established to compare\nPage 9/21\nand analyze the effects of SO4•− and HO• on ROS. As shown in Fig. 4a, compared with the control group,\nROS levels increased by 23.2% after UV/H2O2 disinfection, which was lower than that of UV/PS\ndisinfection. This indicates that the presence of SO4•−in UV/PS system promotes stronger oxidative\nstress in cells.\nOrganisms have formed a complete antioxidant system in the long evolutionary process, which is divided\ninto enzymatic antioxidant systems (such as CAT, SOD) and non-enzymatic antioxidant systems (Ahmad\net al. 2020). The enzymatic antioxidant systems indicators are shown in Fig. 4, and the non-enzymatic\nantioxidant system indicators are shown in Table 1. As shown in Fig. 4c, SOD levels by 7.2%, 7.8%, 7.8%,\nand 6.0%, respectively, while CAT levels increased by 25.8%, 34.4%, 13.1% and 15.9%. SOD acts as a\ncentral regulator of ROS levels, capable of catalyzing the decomposition of superoxide into hydrogen\nperoxide and water (Landis &Tower 2005). The level of intracellular superoxide is consistent with the\nchange trend of SOD (Fig. 4b). Specically, compared with control group, UV, UV/PS, PS disinfection\nincreased by 2.7%, 19.4%, 17.5%, and UV/H2O2, H2O2 decreased by 4.6% and 9.7%. These data suggest\nthat SO4•− produced by PS can induce the production of more ·O2− and accelerate cell death. By\ndecomposing hydrogen peroxide, catalase (CAT) is involved in cellular antioxidant defense (Ho et al.\n2004), and shows its importance when peroxidase is depleted (Liochev 2013). The signicant increase in\nCAT levels after UV/PS disinfection indicated that the cells suffered severe oxidative damage.\nAt the same time, Table 1 shows that the non-enzymatic antioxidant system activity of UV/PS is\nsignicantly lower than that of the UV and PS groups. Glycine, L-Glutamate, L-Cysteine, 5-Oxoproline are\npart of Glutathione metabolism. The decrease of glycine, L-Glutamate, 5-Oxoproline levels ultimately\naffects the biosynthesis of glutathione (GSH). Glutathione is a signicant non-enzymatic cellular\nantioxidant that works with selenium-dependent glutathione peroxidase to remove free radicals such\nhydrogen peroxide, hydroxyl radicals, and peroxynitrite (Zilmer et al. 2005). GSH depletion is an early sign\nof the cell death process (Zhang et al. 2021). The decreased activity of the non-enzymatic antioxidant\nsystem also means that the intracellular redox is unbalanced, and the sharp increase of ROS causes the\nenzyme to be inhibited or even inactivated (Wang et al. 2021b).\n3.6 Global visualization of metabolic pathway changes in\nresponse to UV, UV/PS, PS\nThe main metabolites and corresponding metabolic pathways of P. aeruginosa treated with UV, UV/PS\nand PS are shown in Fig. 5. 59 metabolites with signicant differences were screened, mainly involving\ncarbohydrate metabolism, amino acid metabolism, genetic information processing (such as aminoacyl-\ntRNA biosynthesis) and energy metabolism (such as sulfur metabolism) (Fig. 5b). Specically, as shown\nin Fig. 6a-b, glyoxylate and dicarboxylic acid metabolism, sulfur metabolism and Citrate cycle (TCA cycle)\nwere down-regulated, but alanine, aspartate and glutamate metabolism was up-regulated after UV and\nUV/PS disinfection. The results suggest that both UV and UV/PS can defend against external stimuli by\ndown-regulating carbohydrate metabolism and up-regulating protein metabolism.\nPage 10/21\nTo further discover the reason for the increased VBNC cells ratio caused by UV/PS, the metabolite levels\nafter UV and UV/PS disinfection were analyzed (Fig. 6c). KEGG analysis found that the metabolic\npathways signicantly disturbed by UV/PS disinfection mainly included Citrate cycle (TCA cycle),\nGlyoxylate and dicarboxylate metabolism, Alanine, aspartate and glutamate metabolism, Aminoacyl-\ntRNA biosynthesis (P < 0.05). Compared to UV disinfection, it was found that D-Glucose, D-Alanine, and L-\nCysteine all increase signicantly after UV/PS disinfection. D-Glucose mainly affects the glycolysis\npathway, which is the energy source of life and central metabolite in many biological pathways (Korch et\nal. 2013). Liao et al. (2020) found that most energy-dependent physiological activities of bacteria in the\nVBNC state are restricted. Therefore, regulation and redistribution of energy is one of the main factors\nthat keeps bacteria into VBNC state. Moreover, glucose promotes the development of P. aeruginosa\nbiolm, which can provide support and protection mechanisms for cell metabolism and growth (She et al.\n2019). The manufacture of bacterial peptidoglycan, which protects bacteria against osmotic lysis,\nrequires D-alanine (D-Ala) metabolism. (Jiang et al. 2021). The existence of L-Cysteine can maintain\nprotein stability. The up-regulation of these three substances may be one of the material bases for UV/PS\ninduced VBNC bacteria to maintain certain activity.\n4 Conclusions\nThis study investigated the eciency and mechanism of UV/PS disinfection on inactivation of P.\naeruginosa from the perspective of VBNC. Compared with UV, UV/PS disinfection shows a stronger\nbactericidal effect by reducing the number of culturable bacteria, where SO4•− radicals in UV/PS system\nplayed a more important role. From the perspective of VBNC, although UV/PS disinfection induces more\nbacteria to enter the VBNC state with certain potential risk, it prolongs the recovery time of VBNC bacteria\ncompared with UV disinfection. The production of VBNC is more due to HO• than SO4•−, while the lasting\nantibacterial effect is owing to SO4•−. Although UV/PS disinfection leads to the death of bacteria through\nthe increase of cell membrane permeability and oxidative stress, metabolic regulation of typical\ncarbohydrates and typical amino acids causes some bacteria to enter the VBNC state.\nDeclarations\nEthical Approval: All authors s stated that this study did not involve ethical issues. \nConsent to Participate: All the authors agreed to submit the paper. \nConsent to Publish: All the authors agreed to publish the paper. \nAuthor Contributions Statement: Zaihui Huang: Experiment, Writing-original draft, Data curation,\nInvestigation, Methodology; Zheng Qi: Investigation, Methodology, Formal analysis; Chunguang Liu:\nexperiment design, data analysis and writing. \nPage 11/21\nFunding: National Natural Science Foundation of China (42077391) and Guangdong Foundation for\nProgram of Science and Technology Research (2020B1212060053) have funded this research. \nAcknowledgements: We would like to thank Haiyan Yu, Yan Zhang and Zhifeng Li from State Key\nlaboratory of Microbial Technology of Shandong University for help and guidance in GC-MS/MS, Micro-\nRaman Spectrometer and ow cytometry. \nCompeting Interests: The authors have no relevant nancial or non-nancial interests to disclose. \nAvailability of data and materials: The datasets used and/or analyzed during the current study are\navailable from the corresponding author on reasonable request. \nReferences\n1. Achouri F, BenSaid M, Bousselmi L, Corbel S, Schneider R, Ghrabi A (2019): Comparative study of\nGram-negative bacteria response to solar photocatalytic inactivation. Environ Sci Pollut Res Int 26,\n18961-18970\n2. Ahmad P, Alam P, Balawi TH, Altalayan FH, Ahanger MA, Ashraf M (2020): Sodium nitroprusside\n(SNP) improves tolerance to arsenic (As) toxicity in Vicia faba through the modications of\nbiochemical attributes, antioxidants, ascorbate-glutathione cycle and glyoxalase cycle. Chemosphere\n244, 125480\n3. Apel K, Hirt H (2004): Reactive oxygen species: metabolism, oxidative stress, and signal transduction.\nAnnu Rev Plant Biol 55, 373-99\n4. Berry D et al. (2015): Tracking heavy water (D2O) incorporation for identifying and sorting active\nmicrobial cells. Proc Natl Acad Sci U S A 112, E194-203\n5. Bianco A, Polo Lopez MI, Fernandez Ibanez P, Brigante M, Mailhot G (2017): Disinfection of water\ninoculated with Enterococcus faecalis using solar/Fe(III)EDDS-H2O2 or S2O8(2-) process. Water Res\n118, 249-260\n. Bui TKN, Mawatari K, Emoto T, Fukushima S, Shimohata T, Uebanso T, Akutagawa M, Kinouchi Y,\nTakahashi A (2022): UV-LED irradiation reduces the infectivity of herpes simplex virus type 1 by\ntargeting different viral components depending on the peak wavelength. J Photochem Photobiol B\n228, 112410\n7. Campo R, Vassallo A, Rabbeni G, Arancio W, Gallo G, Di Bella G (2021): Reactivation of aerobic\ngranular sludge for the treatment of industrial shipboard slop wastewater: Effects of long-term\nstorage on granules structure, biolm activity and microbial community. Journal of Water Process\nEngineering 42\n. Chen S, Li X, Wang Y, Zeng J, Ye C, Li X, Guo L, Zhang S, Yu X (2018): Induction of Escherichia coli\ninto a VBNC state through chlorination/chloramination and differences in characteristics of the\nbacterium between states. Water Research 142, 279-288\nPage 12/21\n9. De Luca A, He X, Dionysiou DD, Dantas RF, Esplugas S (2017): Effects of bromide on the degradation\nof organic contaminants with UV and Fe 2+ activated persulfate. Chemical Engineering Journal 318,\n206-213\n10. Fu H, Ding L, Zhai J, Wang X (2021): Purication effect evaluation of the designed new volcanic soil\nadsorption material containing bioreactor for eutrophic water treatment. Environ Sci Pollut Res Int 28,\n59821-59833\n11. Gaschler MM, Stockwell BR (2017): Lipid peroxidation in cell death. Biochem Biophys Res Commun\n482, 419-425\n12. Guo L, Ye C, Cui L, Wan K, Chen S, Zhang S, Yu X (2019): Population and single cell metabolic activity\nof UV-induced VBNC bacteria determined by CTC-FCM and D2O-labeled Raman spectroscopy.\nEnviron Int 130, 104883\n13. Helander IM, Mattila-Sandholm T (2020): Fluorometric assessment of Gram-negative bacterial\npermeabilization.\n14. Ho YS, Xiong Y, Ma W, Spector A, Ho DS (2004): Mice lacking catalase develop normally but show\ndifferential sensitivity to oxidant tissue injury. J Biol Chem 279, 32804-12\n15. Hong J-M, Xia Y-F, Zhang Q, Chen B-Y (2017): Oxidation of benzalkonium chloride in aqueous\nsolution by S2O82−/Fe2+ process: Degradation pathway, and toxicity evaluation. Journal of the\nTaiwan Institute of Chemical Engineers 78, 230-239\n1. Jiang Q, He X, Shui Y, Lyu X, Wang L, Xu L, Chen Z, Zou L, Zhou X, Cheng L, Li M (2021): d-Alanine\nmetabolic pathway, a potential target for antibacterial drug designing in Enterococcus faecalis.\nMicrob Pathog 158, 105078\n17. Kaur B, Kattel E, Dulova N (2020): Insights into nonylphenol degradation by UV-activated persulfate\nand persulfate/hydrogen peroxide systems in aqueous matrices: a comparative study. Environ Sci\nPollut Res Int 27, 22499-22510\n1. Korch SB, Stomel JM, Leon MA, Hamada MA, Stevenson CR, Simpson BW, Gujulla SK, Chaput JC\n(2013): ATP sequestration by a synthetic ATP-binding protein leads to novel phenotypic changes in\nEscherichia coli. ACS Chem Biol 8, 451-63\n19. Landis GN, Tower J (2005): Superoxide dismutase evolution and life span regulation. Mech Ageing\nDev 126, 365-79\n20. Li J, Suo Y, Liao X, Ahn J, Liu D, Chen S, Ye X, Ding T (2017): Analysis of Staphylococcus aureus cell\nviability, sublethal injury and death induced by synergistic combination of ultrasound and mild heat.\nUltrason Sonochem 39, 101-110\n21. Liao X, Liu D, Ding T (2020): Nonthermal Plasma Induces the Viable-but-Nonculturable State in\nStaphylococcus aureus via Metabolic Suppression and the Oxidative Stress Response. Applied and\nEnvironmental Microbiology 86\n22. Lin H, Ye C, Chen S, Zhang S, Yu X (2017): Viable but non-culturable E. coli induced by low level\nchlorination have higher persistence to antibiotics than their culturable counterparts. Environ Pollut\n230, 242-249\nPage 13/21\n23. Liochev SI (2013): Reactive oxygen species and the free radical theory of aging. Free Radic Biol Med\n60, 1-4\n24. Lisec J, Schauer N, Kopka J, Willmitzer L, Fernie AR (2006): Gas chromatography mass\nspectrometry–based metabolite proling in plants. Nature Protocols 1, 387-396\n25. Liu L, Chen Z, Zhang J, Shan D, Wu Y, Bai L, Wang B (2021): Treatment of industrial dye wastewater\nand pharmaceutical residue wastewater by advanced oxidation processes and its combination with\nnanocatalysts: A review. Journal of Water Process Engineering 42\n2. Liu Y, Pang X, Song J, Liu X, Song J, Yuan Y, Zhao C (2019): Exploring the membrane toxicity of\ndecabromodiphenyl ethane (DBDPE): Based on cell membranes and lipid membranes model.\nChemosphere 216, 524-532\n27. Liu Y, Ji X, Yang J, Tang W, Zhu Y, Wang Y, Zhang Y, Zhang Y, Duan J, Li W (2022): Degradation of the\ntypical herbicide atrazine by UV/persulfate: kinetics and mechanisms. Environ Sci Pollut Res Int 29,\n43928-43941\n2. Lutze HV, Kerlin N, Schmidt TC (2015): Sulfate radical-based water treatment in presence of chloride:\nformation of chlorate, inter-conversion of sulfate radicals into hydroxyl radicals and inuence of\nbicarbonate. Water Res 72, 349-60\n29. Marjanovic M, Giannakis S, Grandjean D, de Alencastro LF, Pulgarin C (2018): Effect of muM Fe\naddition, mild heat and solar UV on sulfate radical-mediated inactivation of bacteria, viruses, and\nmicropollutant degradation in water. Water Res 140, 220-231\n30. Martegani E, Bolognese F, Trivellin N, Orlandi VT (2020): Effect of blue light at 410 and 455 nm on\nPseudomonas aeruginosa biolm. J Photochem Photobiol B 204, 111790\n31. Moreno-Andres J, Farinango G, Romero-Martinez L, Acevedo-Merino A, Nebot E (2019): Application of\npersulfate salts for enhancing UV disinfection in marine waters. Water Res 163, 114866\n32. Nasi ł owska J, Soko ł owska B, Fonberg-Fr ą czek M (2019): Behavior of Listeria innocua Strains Under\nPressure Treatment – Inactivation and Sublethal Injury. Polish Journal of Food and Nutrition\nSciences 69, 45-52\n33. Pousty D, Hofmann R, Gerchman Y, Mamane H (2021): Wavelength-dependent time-dose reciprocity\nand stress mechanism for UV-LED disinfection of Escherichia coli. J Photochem Photobiol B 217,\n112129\n34. She P, Wang Y, Liu Y, Tan F, Chen L, Luo Z, Wu Y (2019): Effects of exogenous glucose on\nPseudomonas aeruginosa biolm formation and antibiotic resistance. Microbiologyopen 8, e933\n35. Sies H, Berndt C, Jones DP (2017): Oxidative Stress. Annu Rev Biochem 86, 715-748\n3. Tran VN, Park S, Khan F, Truong VG, Jeong S, Lee DH, Kim YM, Kang HW (2022): Collective bacterial\ndisinfection by opto-chemical treatment on mature biolm in clinical endoscope. J Photochem\nPhotobiol B 226, 112367\n37. Tsitonaki A, Petri B, Crimi M, MosbÆK H, Siegrist RL, Bjerg PL (2010): In Situ Chemical Oxidation of\nContaminated Soil and Groundwater Using Persulfate: A Review. Critical Reviews in Environmental\nScience and Technology 40, 55-91\nPage 14/21\n3. Wang L, Ye C, Guo L, Chen C, Kong X, Chen Y, Shu L, Wang P, Yu X, Fang J (2021a): Assessment of\nthe UV/Chlorine Process in the Disinfection of Pseudomonas aeruginosa: Eciency and Mechanism.\nEnviron Sci Technol 55, 9221-9230\n39. Wang M, Ateia M, Awfa D, Yoshimura C (2021b): Regrowth of bacteria after light-based disinfection -\nWhat we know and where we go from here. Chemosphere 268, 128850\n40. Wang W, Wang H, Li G, Wong PK, An T (2020): Visible light activation of persulfate by magnetic\nhydrochar for bacterial inactivation: Eciency, recyclability and mechanisms. Water Res 176, 115746\n41. Wang Y, Wu Y, Wang Y, Xu H, Mei X, Yu D, Wang Y, Li W (2017): Antioxidant Properties of Probiotic\nBacteria. Nutrients 9\n42. Wang Z, Wan Y, Xie P, Zhou A, Ding J, Wang J, Zhang L, Wang S, Zhang TC (2019):\nUltraviolet/persulfate (UV/PS) pretreatment of typical natural organic matter (NOM): Variation of\ncharacteristics and control of membrane fouling. Chemosphere 214, 136-147\n43. Wilkinson MG (2018): Flow cytometry as a potential method of measuring bacterial viability in\nprobiotic products: A review. Trends in Food Science & Technology 78, 1-10\n44. Ye S, Zeng G, Wu H, Zhang C, Liang J, Dai J, Liu Z, Xiong W, Wan J, Xu P, Cheng M (2017): Co-\noccurrence and interactions of pollutants, and their impacts on soil remediation—A review. Critical\nReviews in Environmental Science and Technology 47, 1528-1553\n45. Ye S, Zeng G, Wu H, Liang J, Zhang C, Dai J, Xiong W, Song B, Wu S, Yu J (2019): The effects of\nactivated biochar addition on remediation eciency of co-composting with contaminated wetland\nsoil. Resources, Conservation and Recycling 140, 278-285\n4. Zhang S, Ye C, Lin H, Lv L, Yu X (2015): UV Disinfection Induces a Vbnc State in Escherichia coli and\nPseudomonas aeruginosa. Environmental Science & Technology 49, 1721-1728\n47. Zhang W, Gao J, Lu L, Bold T, Li X, Wang S, Chang Z, Chen J, Kong X, Zheng Y, Zhang M, Tang J\n(2021): Intracellular GSH/GST antioxidants system change as an earlier biomarker for toxicity\nevaluation of iron oxide nanoparticles. NanoImpact 23\n4. Zhang Y, Wei M, Huang K, Yu K, Liang J, Wei F, Huang J, Yin X (2022): Inactivation of E. coli and\nStreptococcus agalactiae by UV/persulfate during marine aquaculture disinfection. Environ Sci\nPollut Res Int 29, 45421-45434\n49. Zheng T, Li J, Liu C (2021a): Improvement of alpha-amylase to the metabolism adaptions of soil\nbacteria against PFOS exposure. Ecotoxicol Environ Saf 208, 111770\n50. Zheng X, Feng M, Wan J, Shi Y, Xie X, Pan W, Hu B, Wang Y, Wen H, Wang K, Cai S (2021b): Anti-\ndamage effect of theaavin-3'-gallate from black tea on UVB-irradiated HaCaT cells by\nphotoprotection and maintaining cell homeostasis. J Photochem Photobiol B 224, 112304\n51. Zhou CS, Wu JW, Dong LL, Liu BF, Xing DF, Yang SS, Wu XK, Wang Q, Fan JN, Feng LP, Cao GL\n(2020): Removal of antibiotic resistant bacteria and antibiotic resistance genes in wastewater\neuent by UV-activated persulfate. J Hazard Mater 388, 122070\n52. Zhuang Y, Ren H, Geng J, Zhang Y, Zhang Y, Ding L, Xu K (2015): Inactivation of antibiotic resistance\ngenes in municipal wastewater by chlorination, ultraviolet, and ozonation disinfection. Environ Sci\nPage 15/21\nPollut Res Int 22, 7037-44\n53. Zilmer M, Soomets U, Rehema A, Langel U (2005): The Glutathione System as an Attractive\nTherapeutic Target. Drug Design Reviews - Online (Discontinued) 2, 121-127\nFigures\nFigure 1\nInactivation of P. aeruginosa after UV, PS, UV/PS, and UV/H2O2 disinfection. Conditions: [PS]0 = [H2O2]0 =\n1 mM, the concentration of bacteria is ∼ 6 × 105 cfu/mL.\nPage 16/21\nFigure 2\nGrowth of P. aeruginosa in 96-well plates after (b) UV, (c) UV/PSand (d) PS treatments. Each dot\nrepresents a well, with OD600 reecting bacterial growth in the dark. Conditions: [PS]0 = [H2O2]0 = 1 mM,\nthe concentration of bacteria is ∼ 6 × 105 cfu/mL.\nPage 17/21\nFigure 3\nMembrane permeability (a) and the activity of MDA (b) of the bacteria after exposure to different\nmethods (UV, UV/PS, PS). Conditions: [PS]0 = [H2O2]0 = 1 mM, the concentration of bacteria is ∼ 6 × 105\ncfu/mL.\nPage 18/21\nFigure 4\nEffects of UV, UV/PS, PS and UV/H2O2-induced bacterial oxidative stress regulatory strategies. (a) ROS\nlevels, (b) Anti-superoxide anion ability (U/L) and (c) SOD and CAT activities in bacteria treated with UV,\nUV/PS, PS. Conditions: [PS]0 = [H2O2]0 = 1 mM, the concentration of bacteria is ∼ 6 × 105 cfu/mL.\nPage 19/21\nFigure 5\nThe (a) main metabolites and (b) corresponding metabolic pathways of P. aeruginosa treated with UV,\nUV/PS and PS. The green box represents the substances detected in this experiment. Conditions: [PS]0 =\n[H2O2]0 = 1 mM, the concentration of bacteria is ∼ 6 × 105 cfu/mL.\nPage 20/21\nFigure 6\nThe MetaboAnalyst 5.0 was used to analyze the pathways of metabolites that were severely inuenced in\nP. aeruginosa. Each point represents a metabolic route, which is colored based on the log of the P-value.\nThe larger the effect, the redder the hue, and the degree of inuence determines the magnitude. (a)\nMetabolic pathways affected by UV treatment compared to the control group, (b) Metabolic pathways\naffected by UV/PS treatment compared to the control group, (c) Metabolic pathways and metabolites\nPage 21/21\naffected by UV/PS treatment compared to UV, the blue represents the down-regulation of the substance,\nand the yellow represents the up-regulation of the substance, different colored lines represent different\nmetabolic pathways.\nSupplementary Files\nThis is a list of supplementary les associated with this preprint. Click to download.\nSupportingInformation.docx",
