  "content": "Vol.:(0123456789)1 3\nEnvironmental Science and Pollution Research (2023) 30:65933–65946 \nhttps://doi.org/10.1007/s11356-023-26919-0\nRESEARCH ARTICLE\nImpact of COVID‑19 pandemic on cultural ecosystem services \nfrom urban green spaces: a case from English Bazar Urban \nAgglomeration, Eastern India\nArijit Das1 · Manob Das1  · Swarnali Saha1 · Paulo Pereira2\nReceived: 16 May 2022 / Accepted: 5 April 2023 / Published online: 24 April 2023 \n© The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer-Verlag GmbH Germany, part of Springer Nature 2023\nAbstract\nUrban green space (UGS) provides multiple cultural ecosystem services (CES) and enhances people’s physical and mental \nwell-being. The importance of UGS was more critical during crisis periods such as the COVID-19 pandemic. Recently, UGS \nhas been under severe threats due to rapid urban expansion and climate change. Particularly, the cities in developing countries \nhave experienced a dramatic loss of green spaces due to land use and land cover (LULC) change. This study aims to assess \nthe impact of the COVID-19 pandemic on CES perception obtained from two public UGS, i.e., Subhankar Sishu Uddyan \n(SSU) and Bandh Road (BR) in English Bazar Urban Agglomeration (EBUA), Eastern India. A survey was performed to \nassess the valuation of CES, CES obtained from UGS, and the use of CES difference during partial (unlock phases) and \nafter lockdown periods. The non-parametric tests were carried out to understand the significant differences between CES \nand UGS perceived by the respondents. Explanatory factor analysis was also performed to find the association among CES \nfor each UGS. The results reported substantial variations of UGS use during partial and after lockdown periods. During \nthe after-lockdown period, the UGS mainly were used for social relations (51%), spending time with relatives and friends \n(25%), spending time with children (11%) in SSU and walking (25%), spending time with relatives and friends (21%), and \nphysical activities (17%) in BR. During the partial lockdown period, a significant difference between SSU and BR was \nrecorded from social relations and health value among all CES. After the full lockdown period, a significant difference was \nobserved in recreation, social relations, health, and aesthetic value. The frequency and duration of UGS use during partial \nlockdown periods substantially increased. About 12% of the total visitor visited UGS daily after the lockdown, whereas it \nwas 4% during the partial lockdown. The study’s findings will assist urban planners and policymakers in understanding the \nneed for UGS to promote the well-being of the citizens.\nKeywords Ecosystem services · Green spaces · COVID-19 · Well-being · Health value\nIntroduction\nUrban green space (UGS) is highly vulnerable to rapid urban \nexpansion and climate change (Bardhan et al. 2016; Xu et al. \n2019). In the last few decades, there has been an increase \nin research focused on the UGS impact on urban population \nwell-being (e.g., Ma et al. 2019; Kothencz et al. 2017; Nath \net al. 2018). Despite the UGS’s importance, the UGS are \nunder severe threat due to rapid urban expansion (Arshad \net al. 2020; Wu et al. 2019), one of the major drivers of \nUGS loss (Girma et al. 2019). In developing countries such \nas India, unplanned urban expansion is considered a severe \nthreat to UGS (Kaur et al. 2021). This decreases access to \ngreen spaces (Arshad et al. 2020) and the ecosystem ser -\nvices (ES) supplied. Due to this, several studies highlighted \nthe importance of including UGS and ES in urban plan-\nning (Kabisch et al. 2015). Urban expansion in developing \ncountries is growing dramatically. This process affects UGS \ntremendously in India, and ES is provided (Govindarajulu \n2014; Sundaram 2011). Although urbanization poses severe \nthreats to UGS, few studies in India focused on ES supplied \nResponsible Editor: Philippe Garrigues\n * Manob Das \n dasmanob631@gmail.com\n1 Department of Geography, University of Gour Banga, \nMalda, West Bengal, India\n2 Environmental Management Laboratory, Mykolas Romeris \nUniversity, Vilnius, Lithuania\n65934 Environmental Science and Pollution Research (2023) 30:65933–65946\n1 3\nby UGS (Ramaiah and Avtar 2019; Kadaverugu et al. 2021). \nMore particularly, the cultural ecosystem services (CES) \nobtained from UGS remain unexplored in India.\nUGS supplies habitats essential to prevent biodiversity \nloss in urban areas (Niemela 1999) that are key to supplying \na vast number of regulating (e.g., air quality and climate reg-\nulation, noise reduction, carbon sequestration, water purifi-\ncation), provisioning (e.g., food, medicinal plants, biomass), \nand cultural (e.g., recreation, landscape aesthetics) ES vital \nto urban dwellers’ well-being (Johnson et al. 2019; Giacchè \net al. 2021; Pinto et al. 2021). Several psychological and \nphysical health benefits can be obtained from UGS, such as \nreduced mental stress, depression, anxiety, physical fitness, \nand social interaction (Jabbar et al. 2021; Reyes-Riveros \net al. 2021). Since establishing the Millennium Ecosystem \nAssessment (MEA, 2005), ES and their contributions to \nwell-being have been assessed from diversified perspectives \n(e.g., quantitative and qualitative methods). CES quantifi-\ncation is challenging since CES perceptions and attitudes \nvary between cultures and must consider intangible values \n(Ko and Son 2018). Since the 1990s, urban CES research \nhas become popular (Hubacek and Kronenberg 2013) and \nwas mainly focused on recreation and tourism (Abildtrup \net al. 2013; de Groot et al. 2010). Very little work has been \nconducted on other CES components such as spirituality, \naesthetic value, and cognitive development since they are \nnon-physical and challenging to quantify (MEA, 2005; Ko \nand Son 2018). Thus, UGS has become one of the critical \nmeasures of quality of life, development of the cities, and \nsustainable development (Zhu and Xu 2021; Claudia et al. \n2022). Notably, the importance of UGS has been well recog-\nnized during crisis periods such as the COVID-19 pandemic \n(Claudia et al. 2022).\nIn December 2019, COVID-19 originated in Wuhan \n(China), spreading rapidly worldwide (Noszczyk et al. \n2022). The World Health Organization (WHO) declared \na global public health emergency on 11 March 2020. \nThe heavily affected countries implemented lockdowns \nto restrict the spread of the disease (Lian and Feng  2022; \nKumari and Toshniwal  2020). The public spaces, GS, \nwere closed to prevent COVID-19 transmission (Benzell \net al. 2020; Noszczyk et al. 2022; Gorzelany et al. 2022). \nIn India, the lockdown was imposed on 25 March 2020, \ncontinuing until 31 May 2020. During this period, strict \nrestrictions on access to public spaces (such as parks) \nwere imposed. After 31 May 2020, unlock phases were \nimplemented from 1 June 2020 and continued until 31 \nMarch 2022). During this un-lockdown phase, many \nrelaxations were provided on access to public spaces. \nThe COVID-19 pandemic outbreak substantially affected \nurban life and UGS usage (Venter et al. 2020). Human \nmobility was strongly restricted (Slater et al. 2020), \naffecting people’s physical and mental health. The limi-\ntations to obtaining CES from UGS were high (Richard \nand WCPO staff  2020; Reynolds and Forgione 2020). \nAccess to public spaces was restricted (Rogers 2020; \nYip and Chau 2020). Recent studies showed that UGS \nsubstantially reduced psychological stress (such as poor \nmental health and post-traumatic stress) that increased \ndue to COVID-19 impacts (Geng et al. 2021; Slater et al. \n2020). Therefore, examining the impact of the COVID-19 \nlockdown on CES supplied by UGS is essential. Thus, \nthis study aims to examine the impact of COVID-19 amid \nthe lockdown on CES supplied by UGS.\nSeveral global studies recognized that the COVID-19 \nlockdown affected CES supplied by UGS (Ugolini et al. \n2020; Mayen Huerta and Cafagna 2021; Berdejo‐ Espi-\nnola et al. 2021). Nevertheless, in the Indian context, \nmost of the study was performed on various aspects of \nCES obtained from UGS, such as benefits from urban \nparks (Basu and Nagendra  2021), UGS with their neces-\nsity (Ramaiah and Avtar  2019), and UGS planning for \nclimate changes mitigations (Govindarajulu 2014). How -\never, to our knowledge, no studies were carried out to \nunderstand the impact of lockdown on the CES obtained \nfrom UGS. The outbreak of COVID-19 primarily affected \nthe use, perception, and attitudes of the people (Grima \net al. 2020; da Schio et al. 2021), and most of the study in \nIndia was performed on the impact of COVID-19 on air \nquality (Das et al.  2021; Naqvi et al. 2021; Sharma et al. \n2020), water quality (Arif et al.  2021), urban vulnerability \n(Mishra et al.  2020), and urban slum vulnerability (Das \net al.  2021), but the impact of COVID-19 pandemic on \nCES obtained from UGS has been ignored and unexplored. \nThus, these gaps in the previous research studies inspired \nus to perform a study on the impact of COVID-19 amid \nthe lockdown on CES obtained from the UGS. This study \nhas immense scope to understand UGS’s role in provid-\ning CES from a diversified perspective during the cri -\nsis periods. Thus, this research aims to assess the impact \nof the COVID-19 pandemic on the CES during partial and \nafter-lockdown periods in English Bazar Urban Agglom-\neration (EBUA), Eastern India. The specific objectives of \nthis work are (1) to understand the use of UGS during \nthe partial lockdown and after the complete lockdown, \n(2) to compare the use of CES during the partial and com-\nplete lockdown, and (3) to find out the association CES \nduring the partial and complete lockdown, respectively. \nFew research questions have been formulated to fulfill the \nobjectives of the study. These research questions are as fol-\nlows: (1) For what purposes were UGS used during partial \nand after-lockdown periods? (2) Is there any difference in \nCES usage between partial and after-lockdown periods? \n(3) To what extent are the CES associated?\n65935Environmental Science and Pollution Research (2023) 30:65933–65946 \n1 3\nMaterials and methods\nStudy sites\nTwo UGS were selected to study COVID-19 impact on CES \n(Fig. 1). These UGS are located in the EBUA, Eastern India. \nEBUA is one of the largest urban agglomerations in West \nBengal, with an area of 28 km2 and a population of more \nthan 0.3 million (Dutta and Das 2019). EBUA comprises \ntwo statutory towns (English Bazar Municipality and Old \nMalda Municipality) and three census towns (Bagbari, \nChhatianmore, and Sahapur). Recently, EBUA experienced \nrapid urban growth, and it was considered the fastest-grow-\ning agglomeration in West Bengal (Dutta and Das 2019). \nThe city experienced a rapid change in vegetation cover, \nwater bodies, and agricultural land due to dramatic land \nuse and land cover change (Shaw and Das 2018; Dutta and \nDas 2019). The average temperature is 26 °C, and the aver-\nage rainfall is around 1500 mm in EBUA (Dutta and Das \n2019). There are very few UGS in English Bazar city, such \nas parks, playgrounds, and green spaces at the city and the \nneighbourhood level. These UGS are widely used for recrea-\ntion, social relations, and physical activities. Some major \nUGS are SSU, BR, Brinda Bani Maidan, Malda College \nGround, Prasanti Uddyan, Maldar Uthon, and Mahananda \nPark. Unfortunately, no large park or GS exist in the city. \nAmong all the parks, SSU and BR are the most popular and \nwidely used UGS and, therefore, selected for this work. SSU \nis located in the city’s center and is a public park maintained \nby the English Bazar municipality. This park is not only used \nFig. 1 Location of the study sites: (a) India, (b) West Bengal, (c) EBUA, (i) SSU, and (ii) BR\n65936 Environmental Science and Pollution Research (2023) 30:65933–65946\n1 3\nby the people within the city, people outside the city visit \nthe park, also SSU is mainly used for social relations and \ncultural activities. Band Road is one the most popular UGS \nin EBUA’s southern part, along the Mahananda river. Urban \npeople mainly use this green space for physical, social activ-\nities and recreational activities. The people use this UGS \nbecause of its location, particularly near the river and beauty.\nCES studied and survey and data collection\nIn this study, six CES categories obtained from UGS \nwere developed following the framework proposed by \nthe Millennium Ecosystem Assessment (MEA, 2005) and \nTEEB (2010). These six CES were categorized into 24 \nsub-CES based on previous works (Barrena et al. 2014; \nNorton et al. 2012; Bieling et al.  2014) (Supplementary \nquestionnaire S1; Section. 1 to 5). The CES studied are \nrecreation, social relations, inspirations, aesthetic value, \nsense of place, and health value. The details of the CES \nare presented in Table  1.\nPreviously, to conduct the questionnaires, we tried to \nunderstand how much the visitors value the CES obtained \nfrom SSU and BR. The visitors were asked to identify the \nsix most relevant.\nTable 1 CES used in this study\nCES Description Reference Subcategories\nRecreations UGS provide recreational facilities out-\ndoors, such as walking and relaxing\nPlieninger et al. (2013), Bieling (2014) UGS provides sufficient recreational \nfacilities [REC1]\nUGS are good for recreational activities \n[REC2]\nUGS contains natural beauty for recrea-\ntion [REC3]\nUGS are attractive [REC4]\nSocial relations UGS provide opportunities to build social \ncohesion and bonding through walking \nand gossiping. It offers opportunities to \nform new social relations with neigh-\nbors and unknown people\nPlieninger et al. (2013), Brown (2012) UGS provides opportunities for social \nrelations [SR5]\nUGSs provide opportunities to interact \nwith neighbors/friends [SR6]\nUGS provide opportunities for new social \nrelations [SR7]\nUGS provide opportunities to build social \ncohesion [SR8]\nInspiration UGS stimulate new ideas, thoughts, and \ncreative expression\nPlieninger et al. (2013), \nBurkhard et al. (2012)\nUGS are ideal for relaxation [IN9]\nUGS are a source of inspiration [IN10]\nUGS are essential for enhancing creativity \n[IN11]\nUGS decreases mental stress [IN12]\nAesthetic value UGS are the source of aesthetic value \ndue to their pristine nature and \nfacilitating opportunities for various \naesthetic pleasures of ecosystems such \nas natural beauty, landscape design, \nand visual satisfaction\nPlieninger et al. (2013), Bieling (2014) UGS attracts me for its landscape beauty \n[AV13]\nUGS attracts me for its beautiful design \n[AV14]\nUGS provides opportunities to experience \nnature [AV15]\nUGS is embraced by lush greenery \n[AV16]\nSense of place The UGS foster a sense of attachment to \nthe places\nBieling (2014) I miss this UGS while moving outside the \ncity [SP17]\nUGS remind my past memories [SP18]\nI am emotionally attached to this UGS \n[SP19]\nUGS are comfortable and relaxing [SP20]\nHealth value UGS provide opportunities for regular \nand continuous activities that enhance \npeople's physical and mental health\nKo and Son (2018) UGS reduces mental stress [HV21]\nUGS provide opportunities for physical \nfitness [HV22]\nUGS provides mental refreshment \n[HV23]\nUGS enhance the quality of life [HV24]\n65937Environmental Science and Pollution Research (2023) 30:65933–65946 \n1 3\nFace-to-face interviews were performed in SSU and in \nBR (N = 215). The visitors were randomly selected from \nthe UGS to evaluate the impact of COVID-19 amid the \nlockdown on the use, attitudes, and perception of CES in \nEBUA. Individual and focus group discussions (compris-\ning five to six people) with the park visitors were con-\nducted as well. In total, 104 individuals were interviewed \nin SSU and 111 in BR. The survey was performed in mid \nof July to 29 August 2021 for partial lockdown (unlock \nphases). During the survey, the respondents were also \nasked about CES use, attitudes, and perception during \nthe after-lockdown periods. In SSU, the survey was con-\nducted one time, i.e., from 4 to 6 pm, and in the case of \nBR, it was carried out during the morning (6 am to 8 am) \nand the evening (4 pm to 7 pm), respectively. The survey \nwas conducted during this time because it is in this time \nthat visitor frequency is high. In the case of BR, this \nGS is mostly used for different purposes in the morning \n(mainly for physical activities) and evenings (for mul-\ntiple reasons), such as walking and spending time with \nfriends. The questionnaires were developed in English. \nHowever, then questions were asked in Bengali Dialect. \nThe UGS has also assessed the frequency of UGS visits \nand duration spent in UGS. The details of the methodol-\nogy are presented in Fig.  2.\nThe respondents valued the six CES and the different sub-\ncategories using a 5-point Likert scale ranging from very \nimportant/highly agree (5) to very low important/highly dis-\nagree (1). The survey was focused on the following aspects \n(Supplementary S2):\n1. Socio-demographic attributes of the visitors\n2. Perceived valuation of CES from UGS\n3. Activities performed at UGS during partial and after \nlockdown periods\n4. Duration and frequency of UGS use during partial and \nafter-lockdown periods\n5. Perception toward CES (subcategories), respectively\nStatistical analysis\nIn this study, several descriptive statistics (median, quar -\ntiles) were carried out. The median refers to the middle most \nvalue of the datasets meaning 50% of datasets are smaller \nor equal to the median, and 50% are higher or higher than \nthe median. The first and third quartiles refer to the 25% \nand 75% of the datasets arranged in increasing order. In this \nstudy, the median and the first and third quartiles have been \ncalculated based on the CES obtained from the UGS. There \nwere variations in the use of UGS during partial and after \nFig. 2 Framework of the entire research work \n65938 Environmental Science and Pollution Research (2023) 30:65933–65946\n1 3\nlockdown. Therefore, it is important to understand the dif-\nference between partial and after-lockdown periods. Thus, a \nMann–Whitney (MW) U test was applied to find differences \nin the CES perception between study sites and partial and \nafter a lockdown in SSU and BR. Significant differences \nwere also considered at a p value < 0.05. A factor analysis \n(FA) was performed to determine the association between \nCES for each UGS during partial and after lockdown peri-\nods. FA was performed based on the correlation matrix. \nAll the statistical analysis was carried out with SPSS 22. \nFigures were created using online plotly (https:// chart- stu-\ndio. plotly. com/).\nResults\nUGS visitors’ profile\nThis study surveyed 55% of males and 45% of females from \nSSU and 61% of males and 39% of females from BR. The \ndetails socio-demographic profile of the respondents is \npresented in Table  2. In SSU, the majority of the visitors \nwere 20–30 years old (76%), followed by 30–40 years old \n(13%), 40–50 years old(9%), and above 50 years old (2%). \nOn the other hand, in BR, most of the visitors belonged to \nthe age group of 20–30 years (58%), followed by 30–40 years \n(19%), 40 to 50 years (11%), and above 50 years (12%). \nThe majority of the respondents had high school (class 8 to \n12) (32%) level followed by Bachelor (30.5%), elementary \n(from class 1 to 7) (23.5%), and above master (13.5%). The \neducational status of the visitors was different in the two \nUGS. In SSU, 62% of visitors were students, 8% worked \nas government employees, 14% were business people, and \n16% were domestic. In BR, 49% of visitors were students, \n21% were housewives, 19.0% were business people, and \n11.0% worked as government employees (Supplementary \nTable S1).\nExcept for education level, recreation was significantly \nhigher in BR for gender, education level, and occupation. \nRegarding social relations, except for age, all the remaining \nvariables were significantly higher in SSU than in BR. \nInspiration and aesthetic value were significantly higher in \nBR than in SSU in all the variables. Sense of place was \nsignificantly higher in BR than in SSU for gender and \noccupation. Finally, the health value was significantly higher \nin BR than in SSU in all the studied variables (Table 2).\nTable 2 Difference in socio-demographic characteristics of the visitor toward CES\nThe median (1st quartile, 3rd quartile) values are based on a Likert scale ranging from 1 (low importance) to 5 (very high importance). Different \nletters represent significant differences at *p < 0.05, **p < 0.01, and ***p < 0.001. ns, non-significant differences at p < 0.05\nSSU Subhankar Sishu Uddyan, BR Bandh Road\na 1: Male, 2: Female\nb 1: 20–30, 2: 30–40, 3: 40–50, 4: > 50\nc 1: Elementary, 2:High School, 3: Bachelor, 4: above the Master\nd 1: Students, 2: Government Employees, 3: Businessmen, 4: Housewives\nCES UGS Gendera Ageb Educational levelc Occupationd\nRecreation (REC) SSU 4.2 (3.97, 4.27) 2.49 (2.31, 2.84) 3.20 (2.97, 3.51) 3.14 (2.88, 3.37)\nBR 4.6 (4.44, 4.71) 4.48 (4.38, 4.52) 3.16 (2.93, 3.23) 4.51 (4.35, 4.66)\nMW p value * ** ns **\nSocial relation (SR) SSU 3.83 (3.56, 3.99) 2.40 (2.20, 2.60) 3.43 (3.13, 3.70) 2.24 (2.16, 2.44)\nBR 2.91 (2.65, 3.22) 2.48 (2.08, 2.64) 1.37 (1.08, 1.87) 1.96 (1.71, 2.17)\nMW p value ** ns ** **\nInspiration (IN) SSU 1.9 (1.77, 2.15) 2.35 (2.24, 2.79) 2.41 (2.08, 2.68) 2.60 (2.44, 2.95)\nBR 3.67 (3.43, 4.17) 3.78 (3.44, 4.10) 3.59 (3.40, 3.76) 3.42 (3.19, 3.68)\nMW p value ** ** ** **\nAesthetic value (AV) SSU 3.05 (2.8, 3.33) 2.7 (2.55, 2.9) 2.90 (2.65, 3.17) 3.07 (2.81, 3.27)\nBR 4.80 (4.69, 4.88) 4.51 (4.28, 4.63) 3.45 (3.25, 3.66) 4.27 (4.17, 4.38)\nMW p value ** ** ** **\nSense of place (SP) SSU 2.86 (2.69, 2.97) 2.94 (2.68, 3.09) 2.36 (2.25, 2.66) 1.96 (1.79, 2.25)\nBR 3.86 (3.58, 4.01) 3.19 (2.95, 3.38) 2.04 (1.76, 2.43) 3.01 (2.36, 3.30)\nMW p value ** ns ns **\nHealth value (HV) SSU 1.26 (1.10, 1.49) 1.46 (1.26, 1.66) 1.70 (1.59, 2.04) 2.26 (2.01, 2.52)\nBR 4.82 (4.69, 4.87) 4.14 (3.96, 4.22) 3.34 (3.02, 3.53) 3.87 (3.75, 4.03)\nMW p value ** * ** **\n65939Environmental Science and Pollution Research (2023) 30:65933–65946 \n1 3\nReasons and duration of visits to UGS\nDuring the partial lockdown, SSU was mostly used for social \nrelations (37%), followed by spending time with relatives \nand friends (21%), experiencing nature and its aesthetic \nbeauty (15%), and mental refreshment and relaxation (13%), \nrespectively (Fig.  3). On the other hand, during a partial \nlockdown, BR was mostly used for spending time with rela-\ntives and friends (29%), followed by walking (21%), physi-\ncal activities (14%), fresh environment (10%), and mental \nrefreshment and relaxation (8%). During the partial lock -\ndown, about 43% of visitors visited UGS less than 1 time in \na month, followed by once a week (22.5%) and 1 to 4 times \na month, respectively. Most of the visitors (32%) spent up \nto 1 h, followed by 1 to 2 h (30%), and 2 to 3 h (22%) during \nthe partial lockdown.\nDuring after lockdown periods, SSU was mostly used \nfor social relations (51%), followed by spending time with \nrelatives and friends (25%) and spending time with children \n(11%). In BR, UGS was mainly used for walking (25%), \nfollowed by spending time with relatives and friends (21%), \nphysical activities (17%), and experiencing nature and its \nnatural beauty (12%). Thus, from the overall result, it was \nreported that UGS was mostly used for social relations \n(27%), followed by spending time with relatives and friends \n(23%), walking (14%), and physical activities (9%) (Sup-\nplementary Figs. S1 and S2). After the lockdown, most of \nthe visitors visited UGS less than 1 time in a month (32%), \nfollowed by 1 to 4 times a month (21.5%) and 2 to 4 times \na week (1%). However, most of the visitors visited BR 2 \nto 4 times a week (28%), followed by less than 1 time in a \nmonth (23%) and daily (19%). After the lockdown, 47% of \nvisitors spent 1 to 2 h, followed by up to 1 h (34%) and 2 to \n3 h (12%).\nComparison difference during partial \nand after lockdown toward CES\nRegarding CES’s subcategories, there was a significant dif-\nference in CES between the two UGS. In recreation, except \nFig. 3 Use of UGS with dif-\nferent activities in SSU: a \nchildren playing in the park, b \nsocial relations, c gathering of \nvisitors in the park, d view of \nthe park and in BR, e gather-\ning of people in the evening, f \npeople spending time with their \nfriends, g physical activities in \nthe morning, and h riverside \nview of the green space\n65940 Environmental Science and Pollution Research (2023) 30:65933–65946\n1 3\nREC1 (“UGS provides sufficient recreational facilities”), a \nsignificant difference was observed for all the recreational \nCES (REC2, REC3, and REC4). For social relations, a \nsignificant difference was reported from SR5 (“UGS pro-\nvides opportunities for social relation”), SR7 (“UGS pro-\nvides opportunities to new social relations”), SR8 (“UGS \nprovides opportunities to build social cohesion”), and SR6 \n(“UGS provides opportunities to interact with neighbors/\nfriends”). Among inspiration CES, a significant difference \nduring partial lockdown was found for IN10 (“UGS are a \nsource of inspirations”) and IN11 (“UGS are important for \nenhancing creativity”). Sense of place had the only signifi-\ncant difference for SP20 (“UGS are comfortable and relax-\ning”). Regarding aesthetic value, a significant difference was \nreported between AV13 (“UGS attracts me for its landscape \nbeauty”) and AV15 (“UGS are embraced by lush greenery”). \nThere were also significant differences between CES within \nSSU and BR during the partial lockdown period (Table  3).\nThere was also a significant variation of CES values \nbetween SSU and BR after lockdown periods. In the case of \nrecreation and social relations, a significant difference was \nrecorded from all the CES. Among all the CES under inspi-\nrations, a significant difference was found for IN9 (“UGS \nare ideal for relaxation”), IN11 (“UGS are important for \nenhancing creativity”) and IN12 (“UGS decreases mental \nstress”). There was also a significant difference in aesthetic \nvalue between SSU and BR for AV13 (“UGS attracts me \nfor its landscape beauty”), AV15 (“UGS provides opportu-\nnities to experience nature”) (p > 0.05), and AV16 (“UGS \nare embraced by lush greenery”). In the case of sense of \nplace, there was a significant difference between SSU and \nBR during after lockdown periods for SP17 (“I miss this \nUGS while moving outside the city”), SP18 (“UGS reminds \nmy past memories”), and SP19 (“I am emotionally attached \nwith this UGS”).\nLastly, in the case of health, a significant difference was \nrecorded from HV21 (“UGS reduces mental stresses”). \nHV22 (“UGS provides opportunities for physical fitness”) \nand HV23 (“UGS provides mental refreshment”), respec-\ntively. The Tukey post hoc test showed significant differ -\nences among CES within SSU and BR (Table 4). Significant \ndifferences between SSU and BR were found because BR \nwas provided more CES during partial and after lockdown.\nMultivariate analysis\nFactor analysis (FA) was performed for each UGS during \npartial and after-lockdown periods. The exploratory FA \nloading for two UGS is detailed in Supplementary Tables S2 \nand S3. During the partial lockdown, factor 1 was explained \nby social relations (SR5), aesthetic value (AV16), and sense \nof place (SP17 and SP20). In contrast, factor 2 was formed \nby recreation (REC3), inspirations (IN9 and IN10), aesthetic \nvalue (AV14), and sense of place (SP19) for SSU. Although \nsix groups can be identified, the CES parameters were scat-\ntered in this case. The first group, CES for SSU, includes \nSR5, SR7 (social relations), and REC4 (recreations). \nAnother group was formed with IN9 (inspiration) and HV21 \n(health value). The third group was composed of SR8 (social \nrelation), IN12 (inspiration), and REC1 (recreation), and the \nfourth group was formed by SR6 (social relations) and SP19 \n(sense of place) (Fig. 4).\nIn the case of BR, factor 1 was explained by social rela-\ntions (SR5, SR6, and SR8), inspirations (IN11 and IN12), \nand health value (HV23), respectively. Factor 2 was formed \nby social relations (SR6 and SR7), inspirations (IN10 and \nIN11), aesthetic value (AV14 and AV15), and health value \n(HV22). In the case of BR, three groups can be identified. \nSP20, IN9, REC4, and REC18 formed the first group. This \nTable 3 Visitors’ perception according to CES subcategory obtained \nduring the partial lockdown (median (1st quartile, 3rd quartile))\nValues are based on a Likert scale ranging from 1 (disagree) to 5 \n(strongly agree). *Significant differences at p < 0.05. ns, non-signifi-\ncant differences at p < 0.05. The details of the abbreviations are pre-\nsented in the Supplementary section (S2)\nSSU SubhankarSishuUddyan, BR Bandh Road\nCES ID SSU BR MW p value\nMedian (1st \nquartile, 3rd \nquartile)\nMedian (1st \nquartile, 3rd \nquartile)\nRecreation REC1 2.5 (2.3, 2.6) 2.8 (2.4, 3.1) ns\nREC2 1.6 (1.6, 2.0) 3.6 (3.5, 4.0) *\nREC3 2.0 (1.7, 2.1) 2.7 (2.6, 2.8) *\nREC4 2.6 (2.5, 2.7) 2.9 (2.6, 3.1) ns\nSocial rela-\ntions\nSR5 3.2 (3.0, 3.4) 2.4 (2.2, 2.6) *\nSR6 2.3 (1.8, 2.6) 1.9 (1.4, 2.6) ns\nSR7 2.8 (2.6, 2.9) 2.1 (1.9, 2.2) *\nSR8 2.1 (1.9, 2.5) 1.4 (1.3, 1.6) *\nInspiration IN9 2.3 (1.6, 2.5) 2.7 (2.3, 3.1) ns\nIN10 1.6 (1.3, 1.6) 2.3 (2.1, 2.5) *\nIN11 1.7 (1.6, 2.0) 2.4 (2.0, 2.6) *\nIN12 2.1 (2.0, 2.3) 2.8 (1.8, 2.9) ns\nAesthetic \nvalue\nAV13 1.6 (1.6, 2.0) 2.8 (2.7, 2.9) *\nAV14 1.8 (1.7, 2.1) 2.5 (1.8, 2.6) ns\nAV15 1.3 (1.2, 1.5) 2.6 (2.3, 2.9) *\nAV16 1.7 (1.6, 2.0) 2.5 (2.1, 2.6) ns\nSense of place SP17 1.9 ((1.5, 2.2) 2.30 (1.8, 2.6) ns\nSP18 1.6 (1.4, 2.0) 2.10 (1.9, 2.2) ns\nSP19 2.2 (1.9, 2.6) 2.20 (2.1, 2.5) ns\nSP20 1.9 (1.4, 2.1) 2.60 (2.5, 3.0) *\nHealth value HV21 2.20 (1.7, 2.9) 2.90 (2.6, 3.0) ns\nHV22 1.2 (1.1, 1.3) 2.80 (2.6, 2.9) *\nHV23 1.4 (1.3, 2.1) 2.50 (2.0, 2.9) *\nHV24 1.8 (1.7, 2.0) 2.60 (2.5, 2.8) *\n65941Environmental Science and Pollution Research (2023) 30:65933–65946 \n1 3\ngroup was inversely related to AV16, SP17, SP19, and \nAV14. Finally, the third group was formed by HV22, REC1, \nand HV21.\nDuring after-lockdown periods, in the case of SSU, factor \n1 was formed by recreation (REC3), social relations (SR8), \ninspirations (IN10, IN11, and IN12), aesthetic value (AV15), \nand health value (HV24). Factor 2 was explained by social \nrelations (SR5), inspirations (IN11), aesthetic value (AV13 \nand AV14), and health value (HV21 and HV24), respec-\ntively. In the case of SSU, the CES services are scattered \ndistributed, and six groups were identified. The first group \nwas formed by SR5, SR6, SR7, and SR8. This group is \ninversely related to REC2, HV22, and AV15. Finally, the \nthird group was composed of three CES parameters with \ndistinctive negative values.\nIn the case of BR, factor 1 was formed by recreations \n(REC4), inspirations (IN11), sense of place (SP17 and \nSP18), and health value (HV21). The factor was explained \nby recreation (REC3), inspiration (IN11 and IN12), and \nsense of place (SP19), respectively. In the case of SSU, three \ngroups of CES can be identified. AV14 and SP17 related to \naesthetic and health values formed the first group. A second \ngroup was formed by social relations (SR5, SR6, and SR8), \nrespectively. A third group was composed of diversified \nCES such as health value (HV23, HV21, HV22), recrea-\ntions (REC1, REC2, REC3, REC4), sense of place (SP19, \nSP20), and inspiration (IN11, IN9, IN12).\nDiscussion and conclusions\nThe study showed that COVID-19 amid lockdown substan-\ntially impacted CES obtained from two UGS in an Indian \nurban agglomeration (EBUA), Eastern India. Valuation of \nCES obtained from the UGS, frequency of use, and duration \nvaries between two UGS. There were substantial variations \nbetween the two UGS toward the valuation of CES during \npartial and after lockdown periods. Both the UGS reported \nthat the most commonly mentioned CES was recreation, fol-\nlowed by social relations, aesthetic value, and inspirations, \nrespectively. During after-lockdown period, relatively higher \nimportance was given to social relations (4.88), followed \nby recreation (4.56), aesthetic value (4.23), and inspiration \n(4.12). In SSU and from BR, relatively higher importance \nwas given to aesthetic value (4.79), followed by health \n(4.77), inspiration (4.58), recreation (4.32), and social rela-\ntions (3.68). In the case of the use of UGS, it was reported \nthat UGS was mostly used for social relations (27%), fol-\nlowed by spending time with relatives and friends (23%), \nwalking (14%), and physical activities (9%). During after \nlockdown period, BR was mainly used for walking (25%), \nfollowed by spending time with relatives and friends (21%), \nphysical activities (17%), and experiencing nature and its \nTable 4 Visitors’ perception according to CES subcategory obtained \nafter the lockdown (median (1st quartile, 3rd quartile))\nValues are based on a Likert scale ranging from 1 (disagree) to 5 \n(strongly agree). *Significant differences at p < 0.05. ns, non-signifi-\ncant differences at p < 0.05. The details of the abbreviations are pre-\nsented in the Supplementary section (S2)\nSSU SubhankarSishuUddyan, BR Bandh Road\nCES ID SSU BR MW p value\nMedian (1st \nquartile, 3rd \nquartile)\nMedian (1st \nquartile, 3rd \nquartile)\nRecreation REC1 3.8 (3.5, 4.0) 4.82 (4.71, \n4.85)\n*\nREC2 1.6 (1.5, 2.0) 4.87 (4.84, \n4.93)\n*\nREC3 3.2 (2.7, 3.5) 4.59 (4.41, \n4.64)\n*\nREC4 3.6 (3.2, 3.8) 4.59 (4.36, \n4.71)\n*\nSocial relations SR5 4.6 (4.5, 4.7) 2.76 (2.57, \n3.15)\n*\nSR6 4.0 (4.0, 4.2) 2.94 (2.68, \n3.21)\n*\nSR7 4.2 (4.1, 4.3) 2.00 (1.70, \n2.15)\n*\nSR8 4.2 (4.0, 4.3) 2.75 (2.65, \n3.13)\n*\nInspiration IN9 2.8 (2.5, 3.1) 4.03 (3.85, \n4.25)\n*\nIN10 3.0 (2.6, 3.5) 3.75 (3.65, \n3.93)\nns\nIN11 2.5 (1.9, 3.0) 4.24 (4.18, \n4.26)\n*\nIN12 2.9 (2.7, 3.3) 4.27 (4.25, \n4.56)\n*\nAesthetic value AV13 2.8 (2.7, 3.2) 4.24 (4.21, \n4.31)\n*\nAV14 3.1 (2.7, 3.3) 4.10 (3.76, \n4.47)\nns\nAV15 2.2 (2.0, 2.6) 4.42 (4.24, \n4.55)\n*\nAV16 2.7 (2.6, 2.8) 3.48 (3.27, \n3.60)\nns\nSense of place SP17 3.2 (3.0, 3.5) 4.06 (3.65, \n4.33)\n*\nSP18 3.5 (3.0, 3.5) 4.29 (3.96, \n4.49)\n*\nSP19 3.3 (3.0, 3.5) 4.32 (4.26, \n4.49)\n*\nSP20 3.6 (3.5, 3.7) 4.06 (3.73, \n4.27)\nns\nHealth value HV21 2.6 (2.3, 2.8) 4.60 (4.54, \n4.67)\n*\nHV22 1.8 (1.6, 2.1) 4.76 (4.68, \n4.84)\n*\nHV23 3.6 (3.3, 3.9) 4.36 (4.25, \n4.47)\n*\nHV24 3.4 (2.7, 3.6) 3.60 (3.54, \n4.11)\nns\n65942 Environmental Science and Pollution Research (2023) 30:65933–65946\n1 3\nnatural beauty (12%). The frequency and duration of spend-\ning time in UGS increased after lockdown periods compared \nto partial lockdown periods. For example, after lockdown \nperiods, about 31% of the total respondents visited UGS 1 to \n4 times (16% during partial lockdown periods) in SSU. Simi-\nlarly, 28% of the respondents in BR visited UGS 2 to 4 times \nweekly (19% during partial lockdown periods). As per the \nperception toward the subcategories of CES, it was observed \nthat about 70 to 60% of the total visitors reported that BR \nprovides sufficient recreational facilities, and these UGS are \ngood for walking and physical exercise, respectively. Only \n40% of visitors were satisfied with the recreational services \nprovided by SSS. The visitors were less satisfied with the \nrecreation due to mainly being more diminutive in size and \ngathering of people. Thus, from the study, it was well rec-\nognized that CES obtained from the UGS plays a significant \nrole, and visitors depend highly on UGS for various CES.\nFrom the results of the study, it was well documented \nthat there were differences in CES between UGS over the \nsocio-demographic attributes in EBUA. This finding was \nFig. 4 Relation of PCA factor \n1 and factor 2. a During partial \nand b after the lockdown period \nfrom SSU and c during partial \nand d after the lockdown period \nfrom BR. The details of the \nabbreviations are presented in \nthe Supplementary section (S2)\n65943Environmental Science and Pollution Research (2023) 30:65933–65946 \n1 3\nsimilar to other studies (Pinto et al. 2021). For the UGS, age, \ngender, and educational status had significant differences. \nPrevious works also identified this (Wen et al. 2020; Riech-\ners and Barkmann 2018; Sun et al. 2019). The findings of the \nstudy suggest that planning strategies must be implemented \nto enhance accessibility to meet social equity (Pinto et al. \n2021). The results showed that about 40% of respondents \nvisited BR due to mainly physical activities, and 45% visited \nBR to enjoy the environment (such as fresh environment, \nesthetic beauty, and mental refreshment) after the lockdown. \nThus, it helped to improve physical as well as mental health. \nIn the previous literature, it was well documented that UGS \nplay a significant role in enhancing mental and physical \nhealth (Lee et al. 2015; Astell-Burt and Feng 2019; Ugo-\nlini et al. 2020; Enssle and Kabisch 2020). According to \nAnnerstedt et al. ( 2012), UGS promote physical activities, \nhelps recover illness faster, enhances general mental health, \nand reduces stress and mental disorders. Mainly, in densely \npopulated areas like EBUA, the contributions of UGS are \nmore crucial (Bateman et al. 2013; Keeler et al. 2019). The \nsurvey result showed that users substantially increased \nafter lockdown periods due mainly to physical activities \nand enhancing mental health. In many recent studies, it \nwas documented that the physical and mental health of the \npeople were affected mainly by UGS during the COVID-19 \npandemic (Xie et al., 2020 ; Ugolini et al. 2020 ). Thus, the \nmulti-functional activities of UGS largely affect the use of \nUGS (Kaczynski and PotwarkaLR 2008; Zwierzchowska \net al. 2018).\nFrom the study’s findings, it was well documented that \nUGS are used for multiple perspectives, and the use of CES \nfrom the UGS substantially increased after lockdown peri-\nods. Now, it is important to compare the findings of the \nstudy with other previous studies. Recently, several studies \nwere carried out to assess the impact of the COVID-19 pan-\ndemic on the attitudes, use, and perception of UGS world-\nwide (Berdejo‐Espinola et al. 2021; Geary et al. 2021; Pouso \net al. 2021). For example, Beckmann-Wübbelt et al. (2021) \nperformed a study in southwestern Germany. They showed \nthat visits to the peri-urban forest (PUF) increased during \nthe COVID-19 pandemic, and PUFs were mostly used for \nenhancing mental health (such as stress reduction). As per \nthe study of Ugolini et al. (2021) in Italy, it was reported \nthat UGS were important for relaxation and physical exer -\ncises during the pandemic periods. Ugolini et al. (2020) also \nstudied multiple countries (such as Croatia, Israel, Spain, \nItaly, Lithuania, and Slovenia). Their findings revealed that \nUGSs were mostly used for physical activities, relaxation, \nand experiencing nature, respectively. Behavioral changes \nduring the COVID-19 pandemic were also reported, such as \nwalking in tree-lined streets (Spain) and small urban gardens \n(Italy) and traveling outside the city to green areas (Lithu -\nania), respectively. Geng et al. (2021) studied the impact of \nthe COVID-19 pandemic on global urban park visits. Their \nfindings reported that the number of park visitors increased \nbefore 16 February 2020 (before the extreme outbreak of \nCOVID-19) compared to prior to the COVID-19 pandemic \nperiods. From the result of the study, it was reported that \nthe frequency and duration spent in UGS increased after \nlockdown periods due to the relaxation of the restrictions. \nThe frequency of visits to UGS increased by 30% after the \nlockdown compared to the partial lockdown period. During \nthe partial lockdown period (in July and August 2020), there \nwas a strict restriction on the use and accessibility of public \nspace use, and there were limited opportunities to visit UGS \nin the city.\nPolicy implications\nThe COVID-19 pandemic allowed realizing the importance \nof UGS for the well-being of the people. It also revealed \nthat the presences of UGS (like urban parks) are crucial \nto enhancing the city dwellers’ quality of life, particularly \nduring crisis periods like COVID-19 amid lockdown. In \nmany previous studies, it was well documented that the \nfrequency of UGS uses substantially increased during the \nCOVID-19 pandemic (Lopez et al. 2020; Beria and Lunkar \n2021). The role of UGS in enhancing physical and mental \nhealth during COVID-19 was well recognized (Lin et al. \n2019; Schipperijn et al. 2010). It denotes the importance of \nUGS during crisis periods, and these UGS were very impor-\ntant for the mental and physical well-being of urban dwell-\ners (Venter et al. 2020; Kleinschroth and Kowarik 2020; \nGrima et al. 2020). In urban areas, city dwellers remained \nlocked at home and spent less time outside. People became \ndisconnected from social interactions and felt lonely. The \nurban dwellers became mentally depressed, and there were \nminimal opportunities for outdoor activities due to the \nlockdown. Thus, importance and dependency substantially \nincreased after the lockdown periods of the COVID-19 pan-\ndemic (Ugolini et al. 2020; Noszczyk et al. 2022). Thus, \nfrom the study’s findings, UGS (parks, gardens, patches of \ngreen spaces, and tree-lined streets) must be promoted to \nenhance urban health and well-being. In developing coun-\nties like India, conservation and management of UGS have \nbecome severe challenges due to rapid unplanned and hap-\nhazard urban expansions (Ramaiah and Avtar 2019; Dhyani \net al. 2018). In the Indian context, it was well reported that \nthere was a dramatic loss of green (vegetation cover) cover \ndue to rapid urban expansions and conversion of green \ncover (vegetation cover) to other land use, respectively \n(Nagendra et al. 2012; Bhat et al. 2017). UGS planning and \nmanagement is one of the great challenges to city planners \nand policymakers in fast-growing, developing cities like \nIndia. Previous studies exposed concerns regarding the loss \nof urban green in cities due to rapid urban expansion and \n65944 Environmental Science and Pollution Research (2023) 30:65933–65946\n1 3\nthe deterioration of environmental quality in Indian cities \n(Sundaram 2011; Govindarajulu 2014). In EBUA, several \nstudies were performed by Dutta and Das (2019), Shaw and \nDas (2018), and Pal and Ziaul (2017), and their findings \nreported that there was a dramatic increase in the urban \narea and rapid transformation of land use and land cover. \nAccording to the study of Dutta and Das (2019), from 1991 \nto 2016, 17% of vegetation cover was transformed into agri-\ncultural land, and 2.33% was converted into a built-up area. \nTherefore, urgent actions must be adopted to sustain urban \nenvironmental quality across India. In the Indian context, \nthe roles of UGS were well documented in previous work \nto promote the urban environment, mitigate urban heat \nisland (UHI) effects, enhance mental health well-being, \nand obtain CES from UGS (Chaturvedi et al. 2013; Raha-\nman et al. 2021; Mukherjee et al. 2017). The status of UGS \n(at city and neighborhood scale) and per-capita availability \nof UGS across the cities of India were assessed to achieve \nurban sustainability and promote urban health well-being \n(Anguluri and Narayanan 2017; Singh 2018; Shekhar and \nAryal 2019). Therefore, effective urban landscape planning \nis urgently required to promote urban green space manage-\nment at the city and neighborhood levels.\nThis study has immense scope to understand UGS’s \nrole in providing CES and its role in crisis periods. This \nstudy is crucial to capture CES’s diverse perspective dur -\ning and after the partial lockdown periods. Despite this, \na few limitations of the study can be reported. First, in \nthis study, July–August 2020 and July–August 2021 were \nconsidered partial (unlock phases) and after-lockdown \nperiods. Second, during the pandemic periods, an online \nsurvey was performed due to a lack of equipment, and min-\nimal responses from the survey were collected to maintain \nCOVID-19 protocols onsite. Thus, a sample for the study \nmay not be sufficient. Third, in this study, the urban green \nspace sites were selected based on the frequency of use and \nimportance of UGS sites. Future researchers must consider \nthe size of UGS and diverse sources of ES to understand \nvisitors’ perceptions of ES better.\nSupplementary information The online version contains supplemen-\ntary material available at https:// doi. org/ 10. 1007/ s11356- 023- 26919-0.\nAuthor contribution MD, AD, and SS conceptualized and designed the \nstudy. MD and SS collected and analyzed the data with the support of \nAD and PP, and wrote the paper. MD and SS interpreted the analysis. \nMD, AD, and PP read and modified the paper. All four have contributed \nextensively for data interpretation and discussions.\nData availability The datasets used during the current study are avail-\nable from the corresponding author upon reasonable request.\nDeclarations \nConsent to participate All participants provided their consent for par-\nticipating in the interview.\nConflict of interest The authors declare no competing interests.\nReferences\nAbildtrup J, Garcia S, Olsen SB, Stenger A 