ury reduction from shark. \nArchivos Latinoamericanos de Nutrición, 51(3), 288–292. \nfreely available via SCIELO; retrieved august 2022.\nClarkson, T. W., & Strain, J. J. (2020). Methyl mercury: Loaves \nversus fishes. Neurotoxicology, 81(1), 282–287.\nDa Silva, J. M., et  al. (2020). Mercury levels in commercial \nmid-trophic level fishes along the Portuguese coast – \nRelationships with trophic niche and oxidative damage. \nEcological Indicators, 116(February), 106500.\nde Boer, J., Schösler, H., & Aiking, H. (2020). Fish as an alter-\nnative protein – A consumer-oriented perspective on its \nrole in a transition towards more healthy and sustainable \ndiets. Appetite, 152, 104721.  https:// doi. org/ 10. 1016/j. \nappet. 2020. 104721. ISSN 0195–6663.\nde Paiva, E. L., Morgano, M. A., & Milani, R. F. (2017). Cad-\nmium, lead, tin, total mercury, and methylmercury in \ncanned tuna commercialised in São Paulo, Brazil. Food \nAdditives and Contaminants: Part B Surveillance, 10(3), \n185–191. https:// doi. org/ 10. 1080/ 19393 210. 2017. 13113 79\nDezfouli, A. B., Salar-Amoli, J., Ali-Esfahani, T., Hosseini, H., \n& Ghanati, K. (2018). Evaluating total mercury and methyl \n Environ Monit Assess (2023) 195:617\n1 3617 Page 24 of 26\nVol:. (1234567890)\nmercury contents in canned tuna fish of the persian gulf. Ira-\nnian Journal of Pharmaceutical Research, 17(2), 585–592. \nhttps:// doi. org/ 10. 22037/ ijpr. 2018. 2234\nDomingo, J. L. (2010). Influence of cooking processes on the \nconcentrations of toxic metals and various organic envi-\nronmental pollutants in food: A review of the published \nliterature. Critical Reviews in Food Science and Nutrition, \n51(1), 29–37. https:// doi. org/ 10. 1080/ 10408 39090 30445 11\nDomínguez-Morueco, N., Pedraza-Díaz, S., González-Caballero, \nM. D. C., Esteban-López, M., de Alba-González, M., \nKatsonouri, A., Santonen, T., Cañas-Portilla, A., & \nCastaño, A. (2022). Methylmercury risk assessment based \non European human biomoni