 {
  "title": "Transportation carbon emission reduction potential and mitigation strategy in China",
  "url": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scitotenv.2023.162074",
  "source": "Science of The Total Environment",
  "journal": "Science of The Total Environment",
  "journal_tier": "top",
  "date": "2023-05-01",
  "category": "literature",
  "department": "其他",
  "region": "国际",
  "quality": "C",
  "summary": "Transportation carbon emission reduction potential and mitigation strategy in China",
  "tags": [],
  "content_fetched": true,
  "pdf": null,
  "cid": "5d54f25fe4e587a2",
  "importance": 5.59,
  "added_at": "2026-10-07",
  "_ev": "ti:c1627e21e9195aeb",
  "quality_src": "B",
  "water_rel": 0.3,
  "kg_terms": [],
  "content_type": "研究文献",
  "kclass": "research",
  "content_class": "academic",
  "gate_status": "keep",
  "gate_reason": "学术文献（top：Science of the Total Environment）",
  "content": "Transportation carbon emission reduction potential and mitigation strategy in China\n - King&#39;s College London \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Skip to main navigation \n Skip to search \n Skip to main content \n \n \n \n \n \n \n King's College London Home \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Search content at King&#39;s College London \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Home \n \n \n \n Profiles \n \n \n \n Research units \n \n \n \n Research output \n \n \n \n Projects \n \n \n \n Student theses \n \n \n \n Activities \n \n \n \n Datasets \n \n \n \n Impacts \n \n \n \n Prizes \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Transportation carbon emission reduction potential and mitigation strategy in China \n Caiquan Bai , Zhijun Chen , Daoping Wang * * Corresponding author for this work \n Geography Physical Geography and Environmental Science Risk, Climate and Society King's Climate Hub Geocomputation & Data Science Centre for Integrated Research in Risk & Resilience \n \n Research output : Contribution to journal › Article › peer-review \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n 117 \n \n Citations\n(Scopus) \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Overview \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Fingerprint \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Abstract \n Effectively reducing transportation carbon emissions is greatly significant to achieve the carbon peaking and neutral goals of China. On the basis of considering regional technology heterogeneity, we employ the parametric meta-frontier approach to analyze the carbon emission performance and reduction potential of the transportation sector in China. Then, we further decompose the emission reduction potential's contributors into removing management inefficiencies and filling technology gaps. The estimated potential carbon emission reductions from transportation sector in China are 12.3 million tons, accounting for 8.4 % of the annual transportation carbon emissions. Specifically, the eastern regions, especially Shandong, Shanghai, and Liaoning have the greatest carbon emission reduction potential; while Qinghai, Jiangxi, and Ningxia have the smallest potential. As the major contributors to the potential emission reductions, filling technology gaps and removing management inefficiencies account for 57.5 % and 42.5 % of the total potential, respectively. Moreover, removing management inefficiencies dominate for the eastern region and filling technology gaps for the central and western regions. Finally, we provide provincial-specific emission mitigation strategies based on the identification of the reduction potential and its contributors. Our policy implications help decision-makers to facilitate the low-carbon development of transportation sector. \n Original language English Article number 162074 Journal Science of the Total Environment Volume 873 Early online date 20 Feb 2023 DOIs https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scitotenv.2023.162074 Publication status Published - 1 May 2023 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Access to Document \n 10.1016/j.scitotenv.2023.162074 https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0048969723006903 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Fingerprint \n \n \n Dive into the research topics of 'Transportation carbon emission reduction potential and mitigation strategy in China'. Together they form a unique fingerprint.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Carbon Dioxide Emission \n Earth and Planetary Sciences \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Emission Reduction \n Earth and Planetary Sciences \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n China \n Earth and Planetary Sciences \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Mitigation Strategy \n Engineering \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Transportation Sector \n Engineering \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Greenhouse Gas Emissions \n Economics, Econometrics and Finance \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Decision Maker \n Engineering \n 33% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Performance and Emissions \n Engineering \n 33% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n View full fingerprint\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Cite this \n \n \n \n APA\n \n \n \n Author\n \n \n \n BIBTEX\n \n \n \n Harvard\n \n \n \n Standard\n \n \n \n RIS\n \n \n \n Vancouver\n \n \n \n \n \n \n Bai, C., Chen, Z. , & Wang, D. (2023). Transportation carbon emission reduction potential and mitigation strategy in China . Science of the Total Environment , 873 , Article 162074. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scitotenv.2023.162074 \n \n \n \n Bai, Caiquan ; Chen, Zhijun ; Wang, Daoping . / Transportation carbon emission reduction potential and mitigation strategy in China . In: Science of the Total Environment . 2023 ; Vol. 873. \n \n \n \n @article{a8ac9111cc8c4ecd946c0d17d13762ad, title = \"Transportation carbon emission reduction potential and mitigation strategy in China\", abstract = \"Effectively reducing transportation carbon emissions is greatly significant to achieve the carbon peaking and neutral goals of China. On the basis of considering regional technology heterogeneity, we employ the parametric meta-frontier approach to analyze the carbon emission performance and reduction potential of the transportation sector in China. Then, we further decompose the emission reduction potential's contributors into removing management inefficiencies and filling technology gaps. The estimated potential carbon emission reductions from transportation sector in China are 12.3 million tons, accounting for 8.4 \\% of the annual transportation carbon emissions. Specifically, the eastern regions, especially Shandong, Shanghai, and Liaoning have the greatest carbon emission reduction potential; while Qinghai, Jiangxi, and Ningxia have the smallest potential. As the major contributors to the potential emission reductions, filling technology gaps and removing management inefficiencies account for 57.5 \\% and 42.5 \\% of the total potential, respectively. Moreover, removing management inefficiencies dominate for the eastern region and filling technology gaps for the central and western regions. Finally, we provide provincial-specific emission mitigation strategies based on the identification of the reduction potential and its contributors. Our policy implications help decision-makers to facilitate the low-carbon development of transportation sector.\", author = \"Caiquan Bai and Zhijun Chen and Daoping Wang\", year = \"2023\", month = may, day = \"1\", doi = \"10.1016/j.scitotenv.2023.162074\", language = \"English\", volume = \"873\", journal = \"Science of the Total Environment\", issn = \"0048-9697\", publisher = \"Elsevier BV\", } \n \n \n \n Bai, C, Chen, Z & Wang, D 2023, ' Transportation carbon emission reduction potential and mitigation strategy in China ', Science of the Total Environment , vol. 873, 162074. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scitotenv.2023.162074 \n \n \n \n Transportation carbon emission reduction potential and mitigation strategy in China. / Bai, Caiquan; Chen, Zhijun ; Wang, Daoping . In: Science of the Total Environment , Vol. 873, 162074, 01.05.2023. Research output : Contribution to journal › Article › peer-review \n \n \n \n TY - JOUR T1 - Transportation carbon emission reduction potential and mitigation strategy in China AU - Bai, Caiquan AU - Chen, Zhijun AU - Wang, Daoping PY - 2023/5/1 Y1 - 2023/5/1 N2 - Effectively reducing transportation carbon emissions is greatly significant to achieve the carbon peaking and neutral goals of China. On the basis of considering regional technology heterogeneity, we employ the parametric meta-frontier approach to analyze the carbon emission performance and reduction potential of the transportation sector in China. Then, we further decompose the emission reduction potential's contributors into removing management inefficiencies and filling technology gaps. The estimated potential carbon emission reductions from transportation sector in China are 12.3 million tons, accounting for 8.4 % of the annual transportation carbon emissions. Specifically, the eastern regions, especially Shandong, Shanghai, and Liaoning have the greatest carbon emission reduction potential; while Qinghai, Jiangxi, and Ningxia have the smallest potential. As the major contributors to the potential emission reductions, filling technology gaps and removing management inefficiencies account for 57.5 % and 42.5 % of the total potential, respectively. Moreover, removing management inefficiencies dominate for the eastern region and filling technology gaps for the central and western regions. Finally, we provide provincial-specific emission mitigation strategies based on the identification of the reduction potential and its contributors. Our policy implications help decision-makers to facilitate the low-carbon development of transportation sector. AB - Effectively reducing transportation carbon emissions is greatly significant to achieve the carbon peaking and neutral goals of China. On the basis of considering regional technology heterogeneity, we employ the parametric meta-frontier approach to analyze the carbon emission performance and reduction potential of the transportation sector in China. Then, we further decompose the emission reduction potential's contributors into removing management inefficiencies and filling technology gaps. The estimated potential carbon emission reductions from transportation sector in China are 12.3 million tons, accounting for 8.4 % of the annual transportation carbon emissions. Specifically, the eastern regions, especially Shandong, Shanghai, and Liaoning have the greatest carbon emission reduction potential; while Qinghai, Jiangxi, and Ningxia have the smallest potential. As the major contributors to the potential emission reductions, filling technology gaps and removing management inefficiencies account for 57.5 % and 42.5 % of the total potential, respectively. Moreover, removing management inefficiencies dominate for the eastern region and filling technology gaps for the central and western regions. Finally, we provide provincial-specific emission mitigation strategies based on the identification of the reduction potential and its contributors. Our policy implications help decision-makers to facilitate the low-carbon development of transportation sector. U2 - 10.1016/j.scitotenv.2023.162074 DO - 10.1016/j.scitotenv.2023.162074 M3 - Article SN - 0048-9697 VL - 873 JO - Science of the Total Environment JF - Science of the Total Environment M1 - 162074 ER - \n \n \n \n Bai C, Chen Z , Wang D . Transportation carbon emission reduction potential and mitigation strategy in China . Science of the Total Environment . 2023 May 1;873:162074. Epub 2023 Feb 20. doi: 10.1016/j.scitotenv.2023.162074 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Powered by Pure Link opens in a new tab , Scopus Link opens in a new tab & Elsevier Fingerprint Engine&trade; Link opens in a new tab \n \n \n All content on this site: Copyright &copy; 2026 King's College London, its licensors, and contributors. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies. For all open access content, the relevant licensing terms apply\n \n We use cookies to help provide and enhance our service and tailor content. By continuing you agree to the use of cookies \n \n \n Log in to Pure Link opens in a new tab \n \n \n \n \n \n King&#39;s College London data protection policy Link opens in a new tab \n \n \n \n About web accessibility Link opens in a new tab \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Report vulnerability Link opens