  "content": "Selective sorption and desorption of DOM in Podzol horizons — DOC and aluminium contents of leachates from a column experiment\n - Research Portal - Wageningen University & Research \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Skip to main navigation \n Skip to search \n Skip to main content \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Research Portal - Wageningen University & Research Home \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Contact & Help \n Link opens in a new tab \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Search content at Research Portal - Wageningen University & Research \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Home \n \n \n \n Profiles \n \n \n \n Research units \n \n \n \n Research output \n \n \n \n Datasets \n \n \n \n Projects \n \n \n \n Press/Media \n \n \n \n Activities \n \n \n \n Prizes \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Selective sorption and desorption of DOM in Podzol horizons — DOC and aluminium contents of leachates from a column experiment \n Sara Ramos dos Santos , Judith Schellekens * , Peter Buurman , Jean Thomas Cornelis , Karen Vancampenhout , Wilson Tadeu Lopes da Silva , Plínio Barbosa de Camargo , Pablo Vidal-Torrado * Corresponding author for this work \n Earth Systems and Global Change \n \n Research output : Contribution to journal › Article › Academic › peer-review \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n 8 \n \n Link opens in a new tab \n \n Citations\n(Scopus) \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Overview \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Fingerprint \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Abstract \n Complexation of dissolved organic matter (DOM) with cations and minerals contributes to the stabilization of carbon in soils, and can enable the transport of metals in the environment. Hence, a proper understanding of mechanisms that control DOM binding properties in the soil is important for major environmental challenges, such as climate change and stream pollution. However, the role of DOM source in those mechanisms remains understudied. Here, we consider poorly drained tropical Podzols as a model environment to isolate effects of aluminium and DOM on sorption and desorption processes in podzolisation. We collected E- and Bh-horizons from a Brazilian coastal Podzol under tropical rainforest to conduct a column experiment, and percolated the columns with DOM collected from a stream (Stream), peat water (Peat), litter (Litter) and charred litter (Char). To quantify sorption and desorption from the columns, leachates were analysed for DOC content, aluminium content, pH, and the amount of fulvic acid relative to humic acid. The results showed large differences in DOC retention between DOM-types, which were consistent over all columns. Retention of DOC in the column varied between 25 % and 92 % for DOM-type Stream, between 33 % and 63 % for DOM-type Peat, between 22 % and 47 % for DOM-type Litter, and between 8 % and 49 % for DOM-type Char. Similarly, desorption from columns with B-horizon material highly differed between DOM-types. Percolation with DOM-types Stream and Peat caused a release of native DOC from B columns that was higher than in those percolated with water only. On the other hand, percolation of B columns with DOM-types Litter and Char caused a net DOC retention. These differences reflect that certain DOM-types hindered desorption, while other DOM-types caused active desorption. The large differences in sorption/desorption between DOM-types implies that changes in environmental conditions may highly influence the fate of soil carbon in Podzols. \n Original language English Article number 162234 Number of pages 10 Journal Science of the Total Environment Volume 872 DOIs https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scitotenv.2023.162234 Publication status Published - 10 May 2023 \n \n \n \n \n UN SDGs \n This output contributes to the following UN Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) \n \n \n \n \n \n \n SDG 13 \n Climate Action \n \n \n \n \n \n Keywords Adsorption Complexation Desorption Humic substances Podzol B-horizon Soil carbon \n \n \n \n Access to Document \n 10.1016/j.scitotenv.2023.162234 Licence: Publisher \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Fingerprint \n \n \n Dive into the research topics of 'Selective sorption and desorption of DOM in Podzol horizons — DOC and aluminium contents of leachates from a column experiment'. Together they form a unique fingerprint.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Podzol \n Earth and Planetary Sciences \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Organic Matter \n Earth and Planetary Sciences \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Desorption \n Earth and Planetary Sciences \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Sorption \n Earth and Planetary Sciences \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Humic Acid \n Chemical Engineering \n 14% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Percolation \n Earth and Planetary Sciences \n 11% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Climate Change \n Earth and Planetary Sciences \n 5% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Complexation \n Earth and Planetary Sciences \n 5% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n View full fingerprint\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Cite this \n \n \n \n APA\n \n \n \n Author\n \n \n \n BIBTEX\n \n \n \n Harvard\n \n \n \n Standard\n \n \n \n RIS\n \n \n \n Vancouver\n \n \n \n \n \n \n dos Santos, S. R., Schellekens, J., Buurman, P., Cornelis, J. T., Vancampenhout, K., da Silva, W. T. L., de Camargo, P. B., & Vidal-Torrado, P. (2023). Selective sorption and desorption of DOM in Podzol horizons — DOC and aluminium contents of leachates from a column experiment . Science of the Total Environment , 872 , Article 162234. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scitotenv.2023.162234 \n \n \n \n dos Santos, Sara Ramos ; Schellekens, Judith ; Buurman, Peter et al. / Selective sorption and desorption of DOM in Podzol horizons — DOC and aluminium contents of leachates from a column experiment . In: Science of the Total Environment . 2023 ; Vol. 872. \n \n \n \n @article{f45d769a3d9e4a9fb41eb6db8f9e2910, title = \"Selective sorption and desorption of DOM in Podzol horizons — DOC and aluminium contents of leachates from a column experiment\", abstract = \"Complexation of dissolved organic matter (DOM) with cations and minerals contributes to the stabilization of carbon in soils, and can enable the transport of metals in the environment. Hence, a proper understanding of mechanisms that control DOM binding properties in the soil is important for major environmental challenges, such as climate change and stream pollution. However, the role of DOM source in those mechanisms remains understudied. Here, we consider poorly drained tropical Podzols as a model environment to isolate effects of aluminium and DOM on sorption and desorption processes in podzolisation. We collected E- and Bh-horizons from a Brazilian coastal Podzol under tropical rainforest to conduct a column experiment, and percolated the columns with DOM collected from a stream (Stream), peat water (Peat), litter (Litter) and charred litter (Char). To quantify sorption and desorption from the columns, leachates were analysed for DOC content, aluminium content, pH, and the amount of fulvic acid relative to humic acid. The results showed large differences in DOC retention between DOM-types, which were consistent over all columns. Retention of DOC in the column varied between 25 \\% and 92 \\% for DOM-type Stream, between 33 \\% and 63 \\% for DOM-type Peat, between 22 \\% and 47 \\% for DOM-type Litter, and between 8 \\% and 49 \\% for DOM-type Char. Similarly, desorption from columns with B-horizon material highly differed between DOM-types. Percolation with DOM-types Stream and Peat caused a release of native DOC from B columns that was higher than in those percolated with water only. On the other hand, percolation of B columns with DOM-types Litter and Char caused a net DOC retention. These differences reflect that certain DOM-types hindered desorption, while other DOM-types caused active desorption. The large differences in sorption/desorption between DOM-types implies that changes in environmental conditions may highly influence the fate of soil carbon in Podzols.\", keywords = \"Adsorption, Complexation, Desorption, Humic substances, Podzol B-horizon, Soil carbon\", author = \"\\{dos Santos\\}, \\{Sara Ramos\\} and Judith Schellekens and Peter Buurman and Cornelis, \\{Jean Thomas\\} and Karen Vancampenhout and \\{da Silva\\}, \\{Wilson Tadeu Lopes\\} and \\{de Camargo\\}, \\{Pl{\\'i}nio Barbosa\\} and Pablo Vidal-Torrado\", year = \"2023\", month = may, day = \"10\", doi = \"10.1016/j.scitotenv.2023.162234\", language = \"English\", volume = \"872\", journal = \"Science of the Total Environment\", issn = \"0048-9697\", publisher = \"Elsevier\", } \n \n \n \n dos Santos, SR, Schellekens, J, Buurman, P, Cornelis, JT, Vancampenhout, K, da Silva, WTL, de Camargo, PB & Vidal-Torrado, P 2023, ' Selective sorption and desorption of DOM in Podzol horizons — DOC and aluminium contents of leachates from a column experiment ', Science of the Total Environment , vol. 872, 162234. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scitotenv.2023.162234 \n \n \n \n Selective sorption and desorption of DOM in Podzol horizons — DOC and aluminium contents of leachates from a column experiment. / dos Santos, Sara Ramos; Schellekens, Judith; Buurman, Peter et al. In: Science of the Total Environment , Vol. 872, 162234, 10.05.2023. Research output : Contribution to journal › Article › Academic › peer-review \n \n \n \n TY - JOUR T1 - Selective sorption and desorption of DOM in Podzol horizons — DOC and aluminium contents of leachates from a column experiment AU - dos Santos, Sara Ramos AU - Schellekens, Judith AU - Buurman, Peter AU - Cornelis, Jean Thomas AU - Vancampenhout, Karen AU - da Silva, Wilson Tadeu Lopes AU - de Camargo, Plínio Barbosa AU - Vidal-Torrado, Pablo PY - 2023/5/10 Y1 - 2023/5/10 N2 - Complexation of dissolved organic matter (DOM) with cations and minerals contributes to the stabilization of carbon in soils, and can enable the transport of metals in the environment. Hence, a proper understanding of mechanisms that control DOM binding properties in the soil is important for major environmental challenges, such as climate change and stream pollution. However, the role of DOM source in those mechanisms remains understudied. Here, we consider poorly drained tropical Podzols as a model environment to isolate effects of aluminium and DOM on sorption and desorption processes in podzolisation. We collected E- and Bh-horizons from a Brazilian coastal Podzol under tropical rainforest to conduct a column experiment, and percolated the columns with DOM collected from a stream (Stream), peat water (Peat), litter (Litter) and charred litter (Char). To quantify sorption and desorption from the columns, leachates were analysed for DOC content, aluminium content, pH, and the amount of fulvic acid relative to humic acid. The results showed large differences in DOC retention between DOM-types, which were consistent over all columns. Retention of DOC in the column varied between 25 % and 92 % for DOM-type Stream, between 33 % and 63 % for DOM-type Peat, between 22 % and 47 % for DOM-type Litter, and between 8 % and 49 % for DOM-type Char. Similarly, desorption from columns with B-horizon material highly differed between DOM-types. Percolation with DOM-types Stream and Peat caused a release of native DOC from B columns that was higher than in those percolated with water only. On the other hand, percolation of B columns with DOM-types Litter and Char caused a net DOC retention. These differences reflect that certain DOM-types hindered desorption, while other DOM-types caused active desorption. The large differences in sorption/desorption between DOM-types implies that changes in environmental conditions may highly influence the fate of soil carbon in Podzols. AB - Complexation of dissolved organic matter (DOM) with cations and minerals contributes to the stabilization of carbon in soils, and can enable the transport of metals in the environment. Hence, a proper understanding of mechanisms that control DOM binding properties in the soil is important for major environmental challenges, such as climate change and stream pollution. However, the role of DOM source in those mechanisms remains understudied. Here, we consider poorly drained tropical Podzols as a model environment to isolate effects of aluminium and DOM on sorption and desorption processes in podzolisation. We collected E- and Bh-horizons from a Brazilian coastal Podzol under tropical rainforest to conduct a column experiment, and percolated the columns with DOM collected from a stream (Stream), peat water (Peat), litter (Litter) and charred litter (Char). To quantify sorption and desorption from the columns, leachates were analysed for DOC content, aluminium content, pH, and the amount of fulvic acid relative to humic acid. The results showed large differences in DOC retention between DOM-types, which were consistent over all columns. Retention of DOC in the column varied between 25 % and 92 % for DOM-type Stream, between 33 % and 63 % for DOM-type Peat, between 22 % and 47 % for DOM-type Litter, and between 8 % and 49 % for DOM-type Char. Similarly, desorption from columns with B-horizon material highly differed between DOM-types. Percolation with DOM-types Stream and Peat caused a release of native DOC from B columns that was higher than in those percolated with water only. On the other hand, percolation of B columns with DOM-types Litter and Char caused a net DOC retention. These differences reflect that certain DOM-types hindered desorption, while other DOM-types caused active desorption. The large differences in sorption/desorption between DOM-types implies that changes in environmental conditions may highly influence the fate of soil carbon in Podzols. KW - Adsorption KW - Complexation KW - Desorption KW - Humic substances KW - Podzol B-horizon KW - Soil carbon U2 - 10.1016/j.scitotenv.2023.162234 DO - 10.1016/j.scitotenv.2023.162234 M3 - Article C2 - 36791854 AN - SCOPUS:85148017567 SN - 0048-9697 VL - 872 JO - Science of the Total Environment JF - Science of the Total Environment M1 - 162234 ER - \n \n \n \n dos Santos SR, Schellekens J, Buurman P, Cornelis JT, Vancampenhout K, da Silva WTL et al. Selective sorption and desorption of DOM in Podzol horizons — DOC and aluminium contents of leachates from a column experiment . Science of the Total Environment . 2023 May 10;872:162234. doi: 10.1016/j.scitotenv.2023.162234 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Powered by Pure Link opens in a new tab , Scopus Link opens in a new tab & Elsevier Fingerprint Engine&trade; Link opens in a new tab \n \n \n All content on this site: Copyright &copy; 2026 Research Portal - Wageningen University & Research, its licensors, and contributors. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies. For all open access content, the relevant licensing terms apply\n \n We use cookies to help provide and enhance our service and tailor content. By continuing you agree to the use of cookies \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Research Portal - Wageningen University & Research data protection policy Link opens in a new tab \n \n \n \n About web accessibility Link opens in a new tab \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Report vulnerability Link opens in a new tab",
