  "category": "literature",
  "department": "其他",
  "region": "国际",
  "quality": "C",
  "summary": "Hydrodynamic cavitation using vortex diode: An efficient approach for elimination of pathogenic bacteria from water",
  "tags": [],
  "content_fetched": true,
  "pdf": null,
  "cid": "315ca6a4d0d149a8",
  "importance": 5.59,
  "added_at": "2026-10-08",
  "_ev": "ti:b9ecf2d0ca34566b",
  "quality_src": "B",
  "water_rel": 0.3,
  "kg_terms": [],
  "content_type": "研究文献",
  "kclass": "research",
  "content_class": "academic",
  "gate_status": "keep",
  "gate_reason": "学术文献（top：Journal of Environmental Management）",
  "content": "Hydrodynamic cavitation using vortex diode: An efficient approach for elimination of pathogenic bacteria from water\n - Queen&#39;s University Belfast \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Skip to main navigation \n Skip to search \n Skip to main content \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Queen's University Belfast Home \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Help & FAQ \n Link opens in a new tab \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Search content at Queen&#39;s University Belfast \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Home \n \n \n \n Profiles \n \n \n \n Research units \n \n \n \n Research output \n \n \n \n Projects \n \n \n \n Impacts \n \n \n \n Datasets \n \n \n \n Activities \n \n \n \n Prizes \n \n \n \n Press/Media \n \n \n \n Student theses \n \n \n \n Equipment \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Hydrodynamic cavitation using vortex diode: An efficient approach for elimination of pathogenic bacteria from water \n Pooja Jain , Vinay M. Bhandari * , Kshama Balapure , Jyotsnarani Jena , Vivek V. Ranade , Deepak J. Killedar * Corresponding author for this work \n School of Chemistry and Chemical Engineering \n \n Research output : Contribution to journal › Article › peer-review \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n 100 \n \n Link opens in a new tab \n \n Citations\n(Scopus) \n \n \n \n \n 1487 \n Downloads\n(Pure) \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Overview \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Fingerprint \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Abstract \n The present study successfully demonstrates greener methodology of hydrodynamic cavitation using rotational flows for disinfection of water. Disinfection of two model microbial strains-gram- negative (Escherichia coli)and gram-positive (Staphylococcus aureus)using vortex diode was evaluated. The removal efficacy was quantified for two different cavitation reactors. Practically complete elimination of E. coli was achieved (99%)after 1 h of cavitation at a pressure drop of only 0.5 bar. However, elimination of S. aureus using vortex diode was observed to be lower in comparison to the removal of E. coli and only 60% disinfection could be achieved under similar conditions, which can be subsequently enhanced up to 98% by increasing pressure drop. The results were compared with another cavitating device that employs linear flow for cavitation, orifice. The reactor geometry has significant impact on the disinfection process and orifice was found to require significantly higher pressure drop (10 bar)conditions for disinfection and for eliminating gram-positive bacteria with high efficiency. A plausible mechanism for disinfection was proposed to elucidate the role of cavitation in cell destruction leading to death of cells through the rupture of cell wall, oxidative damage and possible DNA denaturation. Also, a cavitation model using per pass disinfection was developed that can provide meaningful physical description of the disinfection process as against the conventional first order reaction rate model. This study would provide meaningful insight into cavitation process based on hydrodynamic cavitation for the destruction of both gram-negative and gram-positive bacteria from various water sources, including industrial wastewaters. \n Original language English Pages (from-to) 210-219 Number of pages 10 Journal Journal of Environmental Management Volume 242 Early online date 03 May 2019 DOIs https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jenvman.2019.04.057 Publication status Published - 15 Jul 2019 \n \n \n \n Keywords Cavitation Disinfection Kinetics Pollution Wastewater treatment ASJC Scopus subject areas Environmental Engineering Waste Management and Disposal Management, Monitoring, Policy and Law \n \n \n \n Access to Document \n 10.1016/j.jenvman.2019.04.057 Licence: Unspecified Hydrodynamic Cavitation using Vortex Diode: An Efficient Approach for Elimination of Pathogenic Bacteria from Water Copyright 2019 Elsevier Ltd.\nThis manuscript is distributed under a Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-NoDerivs License (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/), which permits distribution and reproduction for non-commercial purposes, provided the author and source are cited. Accepted author manuscript, 1.82 MB Licence: CC BY-NC-ND \n \n \n \n Other files and links \n Link to publication in Scopus \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Fingerprint \n \n \n Dive into the research topics of 'Hydrodynamic cavitation using vortex diode: An efficient approach for elimination of pathogenic bacteria from water'. Together they form a unique fingerprint.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Industrial Wastewater \n Chemical Engineering \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Reaction Rate Model \n Chemical Engineering \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Reactor Geometry \n Chemical Engineering \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n View full fingerprint\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Cite this \n \n \n \n APA\n \n \n \n Author\n \n \n \n BIBTEX\n \n \n \n Harvard\n \n \n \n Standard\n \n \n \n RIS\n \n \n \n Vancouver\n \n \n \n \n \n \n Jain, P., Bhandari, V. M., Balapure, K., Jena, J. , Ranade, V. V. , & Killedar, D. J. (2019). Hydrodynamic cavitation using vortex diode: An efficient approach for elimination of pathogenic bacteria from water . Journal of Environmental Management , 242 , 210-219. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jenvman.2019.04.057 \n \n \n \n Jain, Pooja ; Bhandari, Vinay M. ; Balapure, Kshama et al. / Hydrodynamic cavitation using vortex diode: An efficient approach for elimination of pathogenic bacteria from water . In: Journal of Environmental Management . 2019 ; Vol. 242. pp. 210-219. \n \n \n \n @article{c507b88c9d054d94bc581bbb4d089926, title = \"Hydrodynamic cavitation using vortex diode: An efficient approach for elimination of pathogenic bacteria from water\", abstract = \"The present study successfully demonstrates greener methodology of hydrodynamic cavitation using rotational flows for disinfection of water. Disinfection of two model microbial strains-gram- negative (Escherichia coli)and gram-positive (Staphylococcus aureus)using vortex diode was evaluated. The removal efficacy was quantified for two different cavitation reactors. Practically complete elimination of E. coli was achieved (99\\%)after 1 h of cavitation at a pressure drop of only 0.5 bar. However, elimination of S. aureus using vortex diode was observed to be lower in comparison to the removal of E. coli and only 60\\% disinfection could be achieved under similar conditions, which can be subsequently enhanced up to 98\\% by increasing pressure drop. The results were compared with another cavitating device that employs linear flow for cavitation, orifice. The reactor geometry has significant impact on the disinfection process and orifice was found to require significantly higher pressure drop (10 bar)conditions for disinfection and for eliminating gram-positive bacteria with high efficiency. A plausible mechanism for disinfection was proposed to elucidate the role of cavitation in cell destruction leading to death of cells through the rupture of cell wall, oxidative damage and possible DNA denaturation. Also, a cavitation model using per pass disinfection was developed that can provide meaningful physical description of the disinfection process as against the conventional first order reaction rate model. This study would provide meaningful insight into cavitation process based on hydrodynamic cavitation for the destruction of both gram-negative and gram-positive bacteria from various water sources, including industrial wastewaters.\", keywords = \"Cavitation, Disinfection, Kinetics, Pollution, Wastewater treatment\", author = \"Pooja Jain and Bhandari, \\{Vinay M.\\} and Kshama Balapure and Jyotsnarani Jena and Ranade, \\{Vivek V.\\} and Killedar, \\{Deepak J.\\}\", year = \"2019\", month = jul, day = \"15\", doi = \"10.1016/j.jenvman.2019.04.057\", language = \"English\", volume = \"242\", pages = \"210--219\", journal = \"Journal of Environmental Management\", issn = \"0301-4797\", publisher = \"Academic Press\", } \n \n \n \n Jain, P, Bhandari, VM, Balapure, K, Jena, J , Ranade, VV & Killedar, DJ 2019, ' Hydrodynamic cavitation using vortex diode: An efficient approach for elimination of pathogenic bacteria from water ', Journal of Environmental Management , vol. 242, pp. 210-219. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jenvman.2019.04.057 \n \n \n \n Hydrodynamic cavitation using vortex diode: An efficient approach for elimination of pathogenic bacteria from water. / Jain, Pooja; Bhandari, Vinay M.; Balapure, Kshama et al. In: Journal of Environmental Management , Vol. 242, 15.07.2019, p. 210-219. Research output : Contribution to journal › Article › peer-review \n \n \n \n TY - JOUR T1 - Hydrodynamic cavitation using vortex diode: An efficient approach for elimination of pathogenic bacteria from water AU - Jain, Pooja AU - Bhandari, Vinay M. AU - Balapure, Kshama AU - Jena, Jyotsnarani AU - Ranade, Vivek V. AU - Killedar, Deepak J. PY - 2019/7/15 Y1 - 2019/7/15 N2 - The present study successfully demonstrates greener methodology of hydrodynamic cavitation using rotational flows for disinfection of water. Disinfection of two model microbial strains-gram- negative (Escherichia coli)and gram-positive (Staphylococcus aureus)using vortex diode was evaluated. The removal efficacy was quantified for two different cavitation reactors. Practically complete elimination of E. coli was achieved (99%)after 1 h of cavitation at a pressure drop of only 0.5 bar. However, elimination of S. aureus using vortex diode was observed to be lower in comparison to the removal of E. coli and only 60% disinfection could be achieved under similar conditions, which can be subsequently enhanced up to 98% by increasing pressure drop. The results were compared with another cavitating device that employs linear flow for cavitation, orifice. The reactor geometry has significant impact on the disinfection process and orifice was found to require significantly higher pressure drop (10 bar)conditions for disinfection and for eliminating gram-positive bacteria with high efficiency. A plausible mechanism for disinfection was proposed to elucidate the role of cavitation in cell destruction leading to death of cells through the rupture of cell wall, oxidative damage and possible DNA denaturation. Also, a cavitation model using per pass disinfection was developed that can provide meaningful physical description of the disinfection process as against the conventional first order reaction rate model. This study would provide meaningful insight into cavitation process based on hydrodynamic cavitation for the destruction of both gram-negative and gram-positive bacteria from various water sources, including industrial wastewaters. AB - The present study successfully demonstrates greener methodology of hydrodynamic cavitation using rotational flows for disinfection of water. Disinfection of two model microbial strains-gram- negative (Escherichia coli)and gram-positive (Staphylococcus aureus)using vortex diode was evaluated. The removal efficacy was quantified for two different cavitation reactors. Practically complete elimination of E. coli was achieved (99%)after 1 h of cavitation at a pressure drop of only 0.5 bar. However, elimination of S. aureus using vortex diode was observed to be lower in comparison to the removal of E. coli and only 60% disinfection could be achieved under similar conditions, which can be subsequently enhanced up to 98% by increasing pressure drop. The results were compared with another cavitating device that employs linear flow for cavitation, orifice. The reactor geometry has significant impact on the disinfection process and orifice was found to require significantly higher pressure drop (10 bar)conditions for disinfection and for eliminating gram-positive bacteria with high efficiency. A plausible mechanism for disinfection was proposed to elucidate the role of cavitation in cell destruction leading to death of cells through the rupture of cell wall, oxidative damage and possible DNA denaturation. Also, a cavitation model using per pass disinfection was developed that can provide meaningful physical description of the disinfection process as against the conventional first order reaction rate model. This study would provide meaningful insight into cavitation process based on hydrodynamic cavitation for the destruction of both gram-negative and gram-positive bacteria from various water sources, including industrial wastewaters. KW - Cavitation KW - Disinfection KW - Kinetics KW - Pollution KW - Wastewater treatment UR - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85065502022 U2 - 10.1016/j.jenvman.2019.04.057 DO - 10.1016/j.jenvman.2019.04.057 M3 - Article C2 - 31039530 AN - SCOPUS:85065502022 SN - 0301-4797 VL - 242 SP - 210 EP - 219 JO - Journal of Environmental Management JF - Journal of Environmental Management ER - \n \n \n \n Jain P, Bhandari VM, Balapure K, Jena J , Ranade VV , Killedar DJ. Hydrodynamic cavitation using vortex diode: An efficient approach for elimination of pathogenic bacteria from water . Journal of Environmental Management . 2019 Jul 15;242:210-219. Epub 2019 May 3. doi: 10.1016/j.jenvman.2019.04.057 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Powered by Pure Link opens in a new tab , Scopus Link opens in a new tab & Elsevier Fingerprint Engine&trade; Link opens in a new tab \n \n \n All content on this site: Copyright &copy; 2026 Queen's University Belfast, its licensors, and contributors. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies. For all open access content, the relevant licensing terms apply\n \n We use cookies to help provide and enhance our service and tailor content. By continuing you agree to the use of cookies \n \n \n Log in to Pure Link opens in a new tab \n \n \n \n \n \n Queen&#39;s University Belfast data protection policy Link opens in a new tab \n \n \n \n About web accessibility Link opens in a new tab \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Report vulnerability Link opens in a new tab \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Contact us",
