 {
  "title": "Economic and environmental efficiency of UK and Ireland water companies: Influence of exogenous factors and rurality",
  "url": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jenvman.2019.03.093",
  "source": "Journal of Environmental Management",
  "journal": "Journal of Environmental Management",
  "journal_tier": "top",
  "date": "2019-07-01",
  "category": "literature",
  "department": "其他",
  "region": "国际",
  "quality": "C",
  "summary": "Economic and environmental efficiency of UK and Ireland water companies: Influence of exogenous factors and rurality",
  "tags": [],
  "content_fetched": true,
  "pdf": null,
  "cid": "6aa14588f9f45fbe",
  "importance": 5.59,
  "added_at": "2026-10-08",
  "_ev": "ti:c76f769ea640d004",
  "quality_src": "B",
  "water_rel": 0.3,
  "kg_terms": [],
  "content_type": "研究文献",
  "kclass": "research",
  "content_class": "academic",
  "gate_status": "keep",
  "gate_reason": "学术文献（top：Journal of Environmental Management）",
  "content": "Economic and environmental efficiency of UK and Ireland water companies: Influence of exogenous factors and rurality\n - Bangor University \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Skip to main navigation \n Skip to search \n Skip to main content \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Bangor University Home \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n English Welsh \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Search content at Bangor University \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Home \n \n \n \n Profiles \n \n \n \n Research units \n \n \n \n Research output \n \n \n \n Projects \n \n \n \n Impacts \n \n \n \n Student theses \n \n \n \n Datasets \n \n \n \n Activities \n \n \n \n Equipment \n \n \n \n Prizes \n \n \n \n Press/Media \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Economic and environmental efficiency of UK and Ireland water companies: Influence of exogenous factors and rurality \n Nathan Walker , Andrew Norton , Ian Harris , Arwel Williams , David Styles \n School of Environmental & Natural Sciences \n \n Research output : Contribution to journal › Article › peer-review \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n 337 \n Downloads\n(Pure) \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Overview \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Fingerprint \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Student theses \n (1) \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Abstract \n For water companies, benchmarking their performance relative to other companies can be an effective way to identify the scope for efficiency gains to be made through infrastructure investment and operational improvements. However, a key limitation to benchmarking is the confounding effect of exogenous factors, which may not be factored in to benchmarking methodologies. The purpose of this study was to provide an unbiased comparison of efficiency across a sample of water and sewage companies, accounting for important exogenous factors. Bias-corrected economic and environmental efficiency estimates with explanatory factors were evaluated for a sample of 13 water and sewage companies in the UK and Ireland, using a double-bootstrap data envelopment analysis (DEA) approach. Bias correction for economic and environmental efficiency changed the rankings of nine and eight companies, respectively. On average, companies could reduce economic inputs by 19% and carbon outputs by 16% if they performed at the efficiency frontier. Variables explaining efficiency were: source of water, leakage rate, per capita consumption and population density. Population density showed statistical significance with both economic (p-value 0.002) and environmental (p-value 0.001) efficiency. Consequently, a rurality factor was defined for each company's operational area, which was then regressed against normalised water company performance data. More rural water companies spend more per property (R2 of 0.633), in part reflecting a larger number of smaller sewage treatment works serving rural populations (R2 of 0.823). These findings provide new insight into methods for benchmarking, and factors affecting, water company efficiency, pertinent for both regulators and water companies. \n Original language English Pages (from-to) 363-373 Journal Journal of Environmental Management Volume 241 Early online date 23 Apr 2019 Publication status Published - 1 Jul 2019 \n \n \n \n \n UN SDGs \n This output contributes to the following UN Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) \n \n \n \n \n \n \n SDG 9 \n Industry, Innovation, and Infrastructure \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Access to Document \n JEnvManManuscriptRevised Accepted author manuscript, 642 KB \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Fingerprint \n \n \n Dive into the research topics of 'Economic and environmental efficiency of UK and Ireland water companies: Influence of exogenous factors and rurality'. Together they form a unique fingerprint.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Ireland \n Keyphrases \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Water Industry \n Keyphrases \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Rurality \n Keyphrases \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Economic Efficiency \n Keyphrases \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Exogenous Factors \n Keyphrases \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Environmental Efficiency \n Keyphrases \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Company Influence \n Keyphrases \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Infrastructure Investment \n Economics, Econometrics and Finance \n 100% \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n View full fingerprint\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Student theses\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Analysing the water-energy nexus: Benchmarking efficiency in water services Walker, N. (Author), Williams, P. (Supervisor) & Styles, D. (Supervisor), 16 Aug 2021 Student thesis : Doctor of Philosophy \n \n File \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Cite this \n \n \n \n APA\n \n \n \n Author\n \n \n \n BIBTEX\n \n \n \n Harvard\n \n \n \n Standard\n \n \n \n RIS\n \n \n \n Vancouver\n \n \n \n \n \n \n Walker, N., Norton, A., Harris, I. , Williams, A. , & Styles, D. (2019). Economic and environmental efficiency of UK and Ireland water companies: Influence of exogenous factors and rurality . Journal of Environmental Management , 241 , 363-373. \n \n \n \n Walker, Nathan ; Norton, Andrew ; Harris, Ian et al. / Economic and environmental efficiency of UK and Ireland water companies: Influence of exogenous factors and rurality . In: Journal of Environmental Management . 2019 ; Vol. 241. pp. 363-373. \n \n \n \n @article{3c2d3a2fbe9445ada6932e25782a479c, title = \"Economic and environmental efficiency of UK and Ireland water companies: Influence of exogenous factors and rurality\", abstract = \"For water companies, benchmarking their performance relative to other companies can be an effective way to identify the scope for efficiency gains to be made through infrastructure investment and operational improvements. However, a key limitation to benchmarking is the confounding effect of exogenous factors, which may not be factored in to benchmarking methodologies. The purpose of this study was to provide an unbiased comparison of efficiency across a sample of water and sewage companies, accounting for important exogenous factors. Bias-corrected economic and environmental efficiency estimates with explanatory factors were evaluated for a sample of 13 water and sewage companies in the UK and Ireland, using a double-bootstrap data envelopment analysis (DEA) approach. Bias correction for economic and environmental efficiency changed the rankings of nine and eight companies, respectively. On average, companies could reduce economic inputs by 19\\% and carbon outputs by 16\\% if they performed at the efficiency frontier. Variables explaining efficiency were: source of water, leakage rate, per capita consumption and population density. Population density showed statistical significance with both economic (p-value 0.002) and environmental (p-value 0.001) efficiency. Consequently, a rurality factor was defined for each company's operational area, which was then regressed against normalised water company performance data. More rural water companies spend more per property (R2 of 0.633), in part reflecting a larger number of smaller sewage treatment works serving rural populations (R2 of 0.823). These findings provide new insight into methods for benchmarking, and factors affecting, water company efficiency, pertinent for both regulators and water companies.\", author = \"Nathan Walker and Andrew Norton and Ian Harris and Arwel Williams and David Styles\", year = \"2019\", month = jul, day = \"1\", language = \"English\", volume = \"241\", pages = \"363--373\", journal = \"Journal of Environmental Management\", issn = \"0301-4797\", publisher = \"Academic Press\", } \n \n \n \n Walker, N, Norton, A, Harris, I , Williams, A & Styles, D 2019, ' Economic and environmental efficiency of UK and Ireland water companies: Influence of exogenous factors and rurality ', Journal of Environmental Management , vol. 241, pp. 363-373. \n \n \n \n Economic and environmental efficiency of UK and Ireland water companies: Influence of exogenous factors and rurality. / Walker, Nathan; Norton, Andrew; Harris, Ian et al. In: Journal of Environmental Management , Vol. 241, 01.07.2019, p. 363-373. Research output : Contribution to journal › Article › peer-review \n \n \n \n TY - JOUR T1 - Economic and environmental efficiency of UK and Ireland water companies: Influence of exogenous factors and rurality AU - Walker, Nathan AU - Norton, Andrew AU - Harris, Ian AU - Williams, Arwel AU - Styles, David PY - 2019/7/1 Y1 - 2019/7/1 N2 - For water companies, benchmarking their performance relative to other companies can be an effective way to identify the scope for efficiency gains to be made through infrastructure investment and operational improvements. However, a key limitation to benchmarking is the confounding effect of exogenous factors, which may not be factored in to benchmarking methodologies. The purpose of this study was to provide an unbiased comparison of efficiency across a sample of water and sewage companies, accounting for important exogenous factors. Bias-corrected economic and environmental efficiency estimates with explanatory factors were evaluated for a sample of 13 water and sewage companies in the UK and Ireland, using a double-bootstrap data envelopment analysis (DEA) approach. Bias correction for economic and environmental efficiency changed the rankings of nine and eight companies, respectively. On average, companies could reduce economic inputs by 19% and carbon outputs by 16% if they performed at the efficiency frontier. Variables explaining efficiency were: source of water, leakage rate, per capita consumption and population density. Population density showed statistical significance with both economic (p-value 0.002) and environmental (p-value 0.001) efficiency. Consequently, a rurality factor was defined for each company's operational area, which was then regressed against normalised water company performance data. More rural water companies spend more per property (R2 of 0.633), in part reflecting a larger number of smaller sewage treatment works serving rural populations (R2 of 0.823). These findings provide new insight into methods for benchmarking, and factors affecting, water company efficiency, pertinent for both regulators and water companies. AB - For water companies, benchmarking their performance relative to other companies can be an effective way to identify the scope for efficiency gains to be made through infrastructure investment and operational improvements. However, a key limitation to benchmarking is the confounding effect of exogenous factors, which may not be factored in to benchmarking methodologies. The purpose of this study was to provide an unbiased comparison of efficiency across a sample of water and sewage companies, accounting for important exogenous factors. Bias-corrected economic and environmental efficiency estimates with explanatory factors were evaluated for a sample of 13 water and sewage companies in the UK and Ireland, using a double-bootstrap data envelopment analysis (DEA) approach. Bias correction for economic and environmental efficiency changed the rankings of nine and eight companies, respectively. On average, companies could reduce economic inputs by 19% and carbon outputs by 16% if they performed at the efficiency frontier. Variables explaining efficiency were: source of water, leakage rate, per capita consumption and population density. Population density showed statistical significance with both economic (p-value 0.002) and environmental (p-value 0.001) efficiency. Consequently, a rurality factor was defined for each company's operational area, which was then regressed against normalised water company performance data. More rural water companies spend more per property (R2 of 0.633), in part reflecting a larger number of smaller sewage treatment works serving rural populations (R2 of 0.823). These findings provide new insight into methods for benchmarking, and factors affecting, water company efficiency, pertinent for both regulators and water companies. M3 - Article SN - 0301-4797 VL - 241 SP - 363 EP - 373 JO - Journal of Environmental Management JF - Journal of Environmental Management ER - \n \n \n \n Walker N, Norton A, Harris I , Williams A , Styles D. Economic and environmental efficiency of UK and Ireland water companies: Influence of exogenous factors and rurality . Journal of Environmental Management . 2019 Jul 1;241:363-373. Epub 2019 Apr 23. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Powered by Pure Link opens in a new tab , Scopus Link opens in a new tab & Elsevier Fingerprint Engine&trade; Link opens in a new tab \n \n \n All content on this site: Copyright &copy; 2026 Bangor University, its licensors, and contributors. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies. For all open access content, the relevant licensing terms apply\n \n We use cookies to help provide and enhance our service and tailor content. By continuing you agree to the use of cookies \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n About web accessibility Link opens in a new tab \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Report vulnerability Link opens in a new tab",
