 {
  "title": "Mercury emission from industrially contaminated soils in relation to chemical, microbial, and meteorological factors",
  "url": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envpol.2019.03.093",
  "source": "Environmental Pollution",
  "journal": "Environmental Pollution",
  "journal_tier": "top",
  "date": "2019-07-01",
  "category": "literature",
  "department": "其他",
  "region": "国际",
  "quality": "C",
  "summary": "Mercury emission from industrially contaminated soils in relation to chemical, microbial, and meteorological factors",
  "tags": [],
  "content_fetched": true,
  "pdf": null,
  "cid": "8557ed72c433ebe5",
  "importance": 5.59,
  "added_at": "2026-10-08",
  "_ev": "ti:549b73e8014a8acb",
  "quality_src": "B",
  "water_rel": 0.3,
  "kg_terms": [],
  "content_type": "研究文献",
  "kclass": "research",
  "content_class": "academic",
  "gate_status": "keep",
  "gate_reason": "学术文献（top：Environmental Pollution）",
  "content": "Mercury emission from industrially contaminated soils in relation to chemical, microbial, and meteorological factors \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n Skip to main content\n DIAL.pr en English Français Log In UCLouvain SSO\n Sign-in with local account Email address Password Log in Home Mercury emission from industrially contaminated soils in relation to chemical, microbial, and meteorological factors \n Mercury emission from industrially contaminated soils in relation to chemical, microbial, and meteorological factors Osterwalder, Stefan ; Huang, Jen-How ; Shetaya, Waleed H. ; Agnan, Yannick ; Obrist, Daniel ; et.al. (2019) Environmental Pollution — Vol. 250, n° 1, p. 944-952 (2019) Files Osterwalder_et_al_2019_EP.pdf Open Access Adobe PDF 1.18 MB Download Details Authors Osterwalder, Stefan Author Huang, Jen-How Author Shetaya, Waleed H. Author Agnan, Yannick UCLouvain Author Obrist, Daniel Author Show more Abstract The Minamata Convention entered into force in 2017 with the aim to phase-out the use of mercury (Hg) in manufacturing processes such as the chlor-alkali or vinyl chloride monomer production. However, past industrial use of Hg had already resulted in extensive soil pollution, which poses a potential environmental threat. We investigated the emission of gaseous elemental mercury (Hg0) from Hg polluted soils in settlement areas in the canton of Valais, Switzerland, and its impact on local air Hg concentrations. Most soil Hg was found as soil matrix-bound divalent Hg (HgII). Elemental mercury (Hg0) was undetectable in soils, yet we observed substantial Hg0 emission (20-1392 ng m-2 h-1) from 27 soil plots contaminated with Hg (0.2-390 mg Hg kg-1). The emissions of Hg0 were calculated for 1274 parcels covering an area of 8.6 km2 of which 12% exceeded the Swiss soil remediation threshold of 2 mg Hg kg-1. The annual Hg0 emission from this area was approximately 6 kg a-1, which is almost 1% of the total atmospheric Hg emissions in Switzerland based on emission inventory estimates. Our results show a higher abundance of Hg resistance genes (merA) in soil microbial communities with increasing soil Hg concentrations, indicating that biotic reduction of HgII is likely an important pathway to form volatile Hg0 in these soils. The total soil Hg pool in the top 20 cm of the investigated area was 4288 kg; hence, if not remediated, these contaminated soils remain a long-term source of atmospheric Hg, which is prone to long-range atmospheric transport. Show more Affiliations UCLouvain SST/ELI/ELIE - Environmental Sciences Show more Citations APA Chicago FWB \n Osterwalder, S., Huang, J.-H., Shetaya, W. H., Agnan, Y., Frossard, A., Frey, B., Alewell, C., Kretzschmar, R., Biester, H., & Obrist, D. (2019). Mercury emission from industrially contaminated soils in relation to chemical, microbial, and meteorological factors. Environmental Pollution , 250 (1), 944-952. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envpol.2019.03.093 (Original work published 2019) \n Publication type Journal article Research article Year 2019 Language English Handle https://hdl.handle.net/2078.5/126268 DOI 10.1016/j.envpol.2019.03.093 Serial Environmental Pollution Volume: 250 Issue: 1 Pages: 944-952 Date: 2019 ISSN: 0269-7491 eISSN: 1873-6424 Publisher Pergamon Kidlington Peer-reviewed Yes Publication status Published Keywords Toxicology Pollution Health Toxicology and Mutagenesis General Medicine Quick links Cookie settings Privacy policy End user agreement Copyright policy Remote access ? Information What is Open access ? Rights & obligations regarding OA About CC licences Help Ask a question Support guides & Tutorials frontend backend",
